Sesame oil chicken.

00:40:10.985 --> 00:40:11.818
Mm.

00:40:11.818 --> 00:40:13.796
Delightful nuttiness of the sesame oil.

00:40:13.796 --> 00:40:14.943
- Yes.

00:40:14.943 --> 00:40:15.825
Super nutty.

00:40:15.825 --> 00:40:16.658
- Super.

00:40:16.658 --> 00:40:18.369
- It's got this hearty feel to it.

00:40:18.369 --> 00:40:22.400
- Oh, yeah, this is hearty as (beep).

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:23.233
How was that?

00:40:23.233 --> 00:40:27.455
- So incredibly juicy and tender and, ugh.

00:40:27.455 --> 00:40:28.555
I'm going to smell the Thai basil,

00:40:28.555 --> 00:40:31.963
because that's their specialty here.

00:40:31.963 --> 00:40:33.245
- Whoa!

00:40:33.245 --> 00:40:34.750
Smells like pesto.

00:40:34.750 --> 00:40:35.583
- Yeah.

00:40:38.455 --> 00:40:39.288
What?

00:40:39.288 --> 00:40:40.539
- That is awesome.

00:40:40.539 --> 00:40:43.809
That is like a salad dressing
to dip your soup into.

00:40:43.809 --> 00:40:47.976
Adam, you gotta try the
chicken in the Thai basil.

00:40:48.825 --> 00:40:50.015
- Ooh.
(Andrew laughs)

00:40:50.015 --> 00:40:51.079
- Want to try some rice?

00:40:51.079 --> 00:40:51.912
- Oh, yes.

00:40:52.901 --> 00:40:55.993
- Chicken, Steven.
- Cheers.

00:40:55.993 --> 00:41:00.082
- The thing I keep getting
blown away by in Taiwan,

00:41:00.082 --> 00:41:02.497
I go to a meal expecting one thing,

00:41:02.497 --> 00:41:05.644
and then there's always
all this bonus stuff

00:41:05.644 --> 00:41:08.555
that just blows my
expectations out of the water.

00:41:08.555 --> 00:41:10.810
- Yeah, and even when they
first started this restaurant,

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:12.245
this was a bonus dish.

00:41:12.245 --> 00:41:13.078
- Right.

00:41:13.078 --> 00:41:14.770
- Right, that's crazy.
- This was a free thing.

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:16.999
- So there's a lot of
herbs added to this soup.

00:41:16.999 --> 00:41:18.414
It tastes like an elixir.

00:41:18.414 --> 00:41:21.145
It just washes away all
of the pain in your body.

00:41:21.145 --> 00:41:23.303
Achy back, I got a soup for that.

00:41:23.303 --> 00:41:24.567
Tired eyes.

00:41:24.567 --> 00:41:25.448
- Soup for that.

00:41:25.448 --> 00:41:26.460
- My foot hurts.

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:27.598
- Throw some soup on there.

00:41:27.598 --> 00:41:28.598
- It's all the same soup.

00:41:28.598 --> 00:41:29.442
It's very convenient.

00:41:29.442 --> 00:41:31.469
Adam, you want to get some of this soup?

00:41:31.469 --> 00:41:33.078
I'm pretending like Adam's over here,

00:41:33.078 --> 00:41:34.373
even though he's over there.

00:41:34.373 --> 00:41:35.206
- Hi.

00:41:35.206 --> 00:41:37.289
(laughs)

00:41:38.671 --> 00:41:41.868
- So our next chicken
location is in Taipei,

00:41:41.868 --> 00:41:44.885
but first we're going to
take a countryside detour.

00:41:44.885 --> 00:41:45.718
- Yep, that's right.

00:41:45.718 --> 00:41:48.040
We're going to a scallion farm in Yilan.

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:51.021
And we're going to be making
our own scallion pancakes.

00:41:51.021 --> 00:41:52.020
Not just cooking them.

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:53.370
We're going to pick them ourselves.

00:41:53.370 --> 00:41:54.658
- Scallions, yeah.
- Yes.

00:41:54.658 --> 00:41:56.949
- It's a chicken episode,
it's a soup episode,

00:41:56.949 --> 00:41:58.798
and now it's a pancake episode.

00:41:58.798 --> 00:42:02.409
(upbeat trumpet music)

00:42:02.409 --> 00:42:05.304
- What you see in front of you
here is a scallion pancake,

00:42:05.304 --> 00:42:07.345
but rewind three hours.

00:42:07.345 --> 00:42:08.207
What did we do?

00:42:08.207 --> 00:42:09.632
- [Andrew] We're in this unique valley

00:42:09.632 --> 00:42:12.281
where you get different temperature winds

00:42:12.281 --> 00:42:13.764
at different times of day,

00:42:13.764 --> 00:42:17.437
which create a nice perfect
humidity for growing scallions.

00:42:17.437 --> 00:42:19.608
So today we've picked
a bunch of scallions,

00:42:19.608 --> 00:42:21.700
cleaned them up, chopped
them, cried a little bit,

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:23.946
and then learned how to
make scallion pancakes.

00:42:23.946 --> 00:42:26.918
And here we have it,
the spoils of our toils.

00:42:26.918 --> 00:42:28.501
- Cheers!
- Cheers!

00:42:30.272 --> 00:42:31.105
- Mm.

00:42:31.105 --> 00:42:32.711
- Oh, yo.

00:42:32.711 --> 00:42:33.544
- Yo.

00:42:35.524 --> 00:42:38.114
Okay, this is one of the best
things I've eaten so far.

00:42:38.114 --> 00:42:39.265
You know what time it is now?

00:42:39.265 --> 00:42:41.360
Time for a Taiwan food fact.

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:43.826
The concept of "xiao
chi," meaning little eats,

00:42:43.826 --> 00:42:46.804
is popular in Taiwan,
basically meaning snacking

00:42:46.804 --> 00:42:49.949
throughout the day, often on
street food, between meals.

00:42:49.949 --> 00:42:50.782
- Of course, yeah.

00:42:50.782 --> 00:42:53.412
In Taiwan, if you only eat
three meals in one day,

00:42:53.412 --> 00:42:54.431
you're just doing it wrong.

00:42:54.431 --> 00:42:57.529
- We're actually on our
way to a snack shop next.

00:42:57.529 --> 00:42:58.574
Inga, where are we going?

00:42:58.574 --> 00:43:00.492
(Inga speaks in foreign language)

00:43:00.492 --> 00:43:02.331
We're going to see Reebok and Julia

00:43:02.331 --> 00:43:03.709
and try their fried chicken.

00:43:03.709 --> 00:43:06.722
(solemn piano music)

00:43:06.722 --> 00:43:08.528
- What makes a perfect
fried chicken to you guys?

00:43:08.528 --> 00:43:12.278
(speaks in foreign language)

00:43:19.475 --> 00:43:20.799
- [Andrew] So what makes your Taiwanese

00:43:20.799 --> 00:43:21.772
fried chicken unique?

00:43:21.772 --> 00:43:25.522
(speaks in foreign language)

00:43:54.672 --> 00:43:56.093
- [Steven] What do you
recommend that we drink

00:43:56.093 --> 00:43:57.497
with the fried chicken?

00:43:57.497 --> 00:44:01.247
(speaks in foreign language)

00:44:15.165 --> 00:44:15.998
- [Andrew] What?

00:44:15.998 --> 00:44:17.292
- This is not what I was expecting.

00:44:17.292 --> 00:44:18.125
- You ready, Steven?

00:44:18.125 --> 00:44:19.278
- I'm ready.

00:44:19.278 --> 00:44:22.296
- This is called dining al fresco.

00:44:22.296 --> 00:44:25.215
- So we've ordered the
recommended sparkling wine.

00:44:25.215 --> 00:44:28.142
Apparently, you only
order by the bottle here.

00:44:28.142 --> 00:44:28.975
- I like that.

00:44:28.975 --> 00:44:30.351
- It's my speed.
- Let's go.

00:44:30.351 --> 00:44:31.184
Cheers.

00:44:33.563 --> 00:44:34.613
- How awesome is this?

00:44:34.613 --> 00:44:36.098
This is some riviera (beep).

00:44:36.098 --> 00:44:38.066
- We got to eat this while
it's hot, so let's go.

00:44:38.066 --> 00:44:39.537
Ooo.

00:44:39.537 --> 00:44:40.890
It smells so good.
- Oh, it's so fragrant.

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:43.723
The garlic, the onion, this basil.

00:44:46.164 --> 00:44:48.152
The meat inside is so soft.

00:44:48.152 --> 00:44:49.883
- It's very pleasing.

00:44:49.883 --> 00:44:50.752
- Very pleasing.

00:44:50.752 --> 00:44:52.263
Like brûléed marshmallows.

00:44:52.263 --> 00:44:53.513
Seriously.

00:44:53.513 --> 00:44:54.989
- Oh man.
- It's wild.

00:44:54.989 --> 00:44:58.066
I thought QQ was only for dumplings.

00:44:58.066 --> 00:45:01.316
- Whoa.
(Andrew laughs)

00:45:02.197 --> 00:45:03.030
- It's so soft.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:05.344
- It's almost like chicken nugget-esque.

00:45:05.344 --> 00:45:06.553
- You know what's crazy about this?

00:45:06.553 --> 00:45:09.610
We've eaten fried chicken in Minneapolis,

00:45:09.610 --> 00:45:11.414
where the restaurant thought,
"You know what's actually

00:45:11.414 --> 00:45:13.627
the best drink to have with fried chicken?

00:45:13.627 --> 00:45:15.029
Sparkling wine."

00:45:15.029 --> 00:45:17.233
And here we are in Taiwan,

00:45:17.233 --> 00:45:19.594
and this restaurant had the same idea.

00:45:19.594 --> 00:45:20.427
- I see, I see.

00:45:20.427 --> 00:45:22.547
- If there's a restaurant
on either side of the world

00:45:22.547 --> 00:45:25.134
that is doing the same
thing, it must be great.

00:45:25.134 --> 00:45:28.884
(speaks in foreign language)

00:45:34.452 --> 00:45:36.175
- I'm scared to eat
this, because I'm scared

00:45:36.175 --> 00:45:37.213
of how much I'll like it.

00:45:37.213 --> 00:45:39.206
- [Andrew] Pork knuckle and radish cake.

00:45:39.206 --> 00:45:40.039
- Wow.

00:45:42.929 --> 00:45:44.087
Ooo.

00:45:44.087 --> 00:45:45.160
Oh, man.

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:47.026
- Watch out, you're going
to push me off my stool.

00:45:47.026 --> 00:45:48.702
- Radish cake is a classic dim sum dish.

00:45:48.702 --> 00:45:50.639
I've eaten this every time I go out.

00:45:50.639 --> 00:45:51.803
You know, they bring around the carts

00:45:51.803 --> 00:45:52.947
and they're all steamed, right?

00:45:52.947 --> 00:45:54.046
It's still hot but it doesn't have

00:45:54.046 --> 00:45:58.014
that break-through-the-crunch
texture that this has.

00:45:58.014 --> 00:46:01.268
- You could maybe liken
it to a potato pancake.

00:46:01.268 --> 00:46:02.101
That's so good.

00:46:02.101 --> 00:46:03.059
- Yeah it's crazy.
- Yeah.

00:46:03.059 --> 00:46:05.859
How should we eat the pork knuckle?

00:46:05.859 --> 00:46:06.713
- [Chef] By hand.

00:46:06.713 --> 00:46:07.756
- [Andrew] By hand?

00:46:07.756 --> 00:46:08.845
Just like an apple?

00:46:08.845 --> 00:46:09.678
- [Chef] Mm-hmm.

00:46:09.678 --> 00:46:10.511
- Okay.

00:46:10.511 --> 00:46:11.564
Kind of looks like a donut.

00:46:11.564 --> 00:46:12.432
- Oh!

00:46:12.432 --> 00:46:13.265
Yours does.

00:46:13.265 --> 00:46:14.098
- Mine does.

00:46:14.098 --> 00:46:14.931
- Cheers.

00:46:19.843 --> 00:46:21.127
- Oh my god.

00:46:21.127 --> 00:46:23.294
This is like a pork donut.

00:46:25.885 --> 00:46:27.239
Wow.

00:46:27.239 --> 00:46:28.351
(Steven laughs)

00:46:28.351 --> 00:46:30.527
- So this is actually
cartilage, but it feels like fat

00:46:30.527 --> 00:46:32.747
with how loosey-goosey it is.

00:46:32.747 --> 00:46:36.535
- The gelatin is the
jelly of this pork donut.

00:46:36.535 --> 00:46:37.368
- Right.

00:46:37.368 --> 00:46:39.483
- And to wash it down, sparkling wine.

00:46:39.483 --> 00:46:40.953
- Oh, thank goodness for this.

00:46:40.953 --> 00:46:43.231
- How awesome is this?

00:46:43.231 --> 00:46:44.064
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:46:44.064 --> 00:46:45.378
- This was really good.

00:46:45.378 --> 00:46:46.466
- Thank you.

00:46:46.466 --> 00:46:50.216
(speaks in foreign language)

00:46:51.482 --> 00:46:53.477
- We are at the Beijing hotel,

00:46:53.477 --> 00:46:56.303
where they serve a boba croissant.

00:46:56.303 --> 00:46:57.325
Yes, you heard me right.

00:46:57.325 --> 00:46:58.743
Boba croissant.

00:46:58.743 --> 00:47:00.236
(imitates whooshing)

00:47:00.236 --> 00:47:02.893
- We got the matcha,
tiramisu and the milk tea.

00:47:02.893 --> 00:47:03.726
Which one do you want?

00:47:03.726 --> 00:47:05.196
- My gut is going toward matcha,

00:47:05.196 --> 00:47:08.145
but I'm going against my gut
and going for the tiramisu.

00:47:08.145 --> 00:47:09.791
- I actually knew that you
were going to pick that one,

00:47:09.791 --> 00:47:11.778
because it has gold flake on it.

00:47:11.778 --> 00:47:12.654
(Steven laughs)

00:47:12.654 --> 00:47:13.758
All right, I'll go milk tea.

00:47:13.758 --> 00:47:15.544
Very satisfying to pick up.

00:47:15.544 --> 00:47:16.423
Cheers.

00:47:16.423 --> 00:47:17.256
- Cheers.

00:47:17.256 --> 00:47:18.171
Are you going to bite into it?

00:47:18.171 --> 00:47:19.547
- Yeah.
- Or open it up?

00:47:19.547 --> 00:47:21.880
- I'm going to bite into it.

00:47:23.111 --> 00:47:25.485
- Oh.
(Steven gasps)

00:47:25.485 --> 00:47:27.427
This is just all of the textures.

00:47:27.427 --> 00:47:28.260
This is nice.

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:30.037
It's satisfyingly squishy.

00:47:30.037 --> 00:47:31.926
- I think there's a word for that, QQ.

00:47:31.926 --> 00:47:34.131
- Ready for a Taiwan food fact, Steven?

00:47:34.131 --> 00:47:35.233
- Taiwan food fact!

00:47:35.233 --> 00:47:36.650
Taiwan food fact.

00:47:37.586 --> 00:47:38.419
- Um.

00:47:38.419 --> 00:47:41.314
- Up until the 1960s, the most
common Taiwanese breakfast

00:47:41.314 --> 00:47:42.718
was rice or congee.

00:47:42.718 --> 00:47:46.228
Buns emerged as a popular
Taiwanese food in the 1960s

00:47:46.228 --> 00:47:49.155
when U.S. trade introduced
wheat into the Taiwanese diet.

00:47:49.155 --> 00:47:51.281
- Shouldn't that fact go
in the dumpling episode

00:47:51.281 --> 00:47:52.181
that we did?

00:47:52.181 --> 00:47:53.429
- Well, it was more about breakfast.

00:47:53.429 --> 00:47:55.484
I just wanted an excuse
to talk about congee,

00:47:55.484 --> 00:47:58.079
because it's just a nice, savory thing

00:47:58.079 --> 00:47:59.267
to have for breakfast.

00:47:59.267 --> 00:48:02.159
- This is a very good croissant.

00:48:02.159 --> 00:48:02.992
- Yeah.

00:48:04.463 --> 00:48:05.858
- I got a cake.

00:48:05.858 --> 00:48:07.041
- [Steven] Oh, my.

00:48:07.041 --> 00:48:09.955
- [Andrew] This boba croissant
is part of a boba festival.

00:48:09.955 --> 00:48:11.692
- Boba fest.

00:48:11.692 --> 00:48:14.734
- And Inga's got the crown
jewel of the festival.

00:48:14.734 --> 00:48:17.794
And now it's time to
take a little train ride.

00:48:17.794 --> 00:48:18.627
- QQ.

00:48:18.627 --> 00:48:19.960
(Andrew laughs)

00:48:19.960 --> 00:48:21.636
You want to hear my boba rap?

00:48:21.636 --> 00:48:22.469
- Yeah.

00:48:22.469 --> 00:48:23.658
- How do you like your boba?

00:48:23.658 --> 00:48:24.491
- Yeah.

00:48:24.491 --> 00:48:25.379
- Eat it like a cobra.

00:48:25.379 --> 00:48:26.212
- Yeah.

00:48:26.212 --> 00:48:27.172
- My name is Yoda.

00:48:27.172 --> 00:48:28.005
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:48:28.005 --> 00:48:30.203
- [Steven] And I like yoga.

00:48:30.203 --> 00:48:31.671
- Now dab.

00:48:31.671 --> 00:48:32.504
- Never.

00:48:32.504 --> 00:48:34.381
- This is a train water
bottle that they sell

00:48:34.381 --> 00:48:35.214
on the train.

00:48:35.214 --> 00:48:37.265
(imitates train chugging)

00:48:37.265 --> 00:48:40.047
So once we get done with this
guy, we'll be in Taichung

00:48:40.047 --> 00:48:42.626
and we'll be eating at our
final chicken location.

00:48:42.626 --> 00:48:45.637
- We're going to go meet
RuiHong at Yan Xiang Lou

00:48:45.637 --> 00:48:47.128
to have Beggar's Chicken.

00:48:47.128 --> 00:48:51.324
(uplifting orchestral music)

00:48:51.324 --> 00:48:53.373
- What kind of cuisine is the food here?

00:48:53.373 --> 00:48:57.123
(speaks in foreign language)

00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:15.883
- Tell us about this dish,
the Beggar's Chicken.

00:49:15.883 --> 00:49:19.633
(speaks in foreign language)

00:49:40.928 --> 00:49:42.253
- What happened to the beggar?

00:49:42.253 --> 00:49:45.042
(speaks in foreign language)
(laughs)

00:49:45.042 --> 00:49:46.734
(speaks in foreign language)

00:49:46.734 --> 00:49:47.567
I hope so.

00:49:47.567 --> 00:49:51.177
(speaks in foreign language)

00:49:51.177 --> 00:49:52.915
- [Andrew] Why bake the chicken in clay?

00:49:52.915 --> 00:49:56.665
(speaks in foreign language)

00:50:21.201 --> 00:50:24.193
How do we get the chicken
out from the clay?

00:50:24.193 --> 00:50:27.943
(speaks in foreign language)

00:50:38.921 --> 00:50:40.264
Which one of us gets to do it?

00:50:40.264 --> 00:50:42.019
- [Steven] I'll do the hammering,
you do the hammer time.

00:50:42.019 --> 00:50:42.852
- What?

00:50:42.852 --> 00:50:44.544
You just automatically assume
that you get to hammer.

00:50:44.544 --> 00:50:48.493
(speaks in foreign language)

00:50:48.493 --> 00:50:50.374
- You can both do it.

00:50:50.374 --> 00:50:51.548
- Two hammers?
- Yeah.

00:50:51.548 --> 00:50:52.446
- Okay.

00:50:52.446 --> 00:50:54.113
- Great.
- Resolved.

00:50:55.972 --> 00:50:57.222
Ooh, thank you.

00:51:00.534 --> 00:51:03.300
Cheers to our final meal in Taiwan.

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:04.414
- Oh, no!

00:51:04.414 --> 00:51:05.247
- I know.

00:51:06.206 --> 00:51:07.039
- Cheers.

00:51:08.952 --> 00:51:11.143
- Since this is our last meal in Taiwan,

00:51:11.143 --> 00:51:12.310
we're blowing it out.

00:51:12.310 --> 00:51:13.143
- [Steven] Let's do it.

00:51:13.143 --> 00:51:15.460
- I saw a little sneak peek
of what's coming upstairs.

00:51:15.460 --> 00:51:17.117
It's going to blow you away.

00:51:17.117 --> 00:51:18.744
So this is winter melon.

00:51:18.744 --> 00:51:20.279
- [Steven] Whoa.

00:51:20.279 --> 00:51:21.362
- Wow.
- Wow.

00:51:25.936 --> 00:51:26.769
- What?

00:51:28.189 --> 00:51:30.393
Look at it coming out,
pouring on the fish's head.

00:51:30.393 --> 00:51:32.611
- These look too real.

00:51:32.611 --> 00:51:34.208
Look at the scales on these guys.

00:51:34.208 --> 00:51:36.419
- Honestly, every time
I've seen a koi fish,

00:51:36.419 --> 00:51:38.488
I've thought they looked
like a tasty fish.

00:51:38.488 --> 00:51:39.352
Cheers, Steven.

00:51:39.352 --> 00:51:40.185
Fish kiss.

00:51:42.898 --> 00:51:45.028
That's a good dumpling.

00:51:45.028 --> 00:51:46.742
- Aw, fish super good.

00:51:46.742 --> 00:51:49.161
- This looks like in
"Hook," when Robin Williams

00:51:49.161 --> 00:51:50.584
is imagining the food.

00:51:50.584 --> 00:51:51.715
Have you seen this movie?

00:51:51.715 --> 00:51:53.392
It's all vibrant colors.

00:51:53.392 --> 00:51:55.315
Do yourself a favor, go watch "Hook."

00:51:55.315 --> 00:51:56.639
You'll have a delightful time.

00:51:56.639 --> 00:51:58.248
You'll be reminded of this moment.

00:51:58.248 --> 00:52:00.665
- I'm going pink for my hair.

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:07.887
We might as well have just
shot all three episodes here.

00:52:09.266 --> 00:52:11.178
- Much like the origin myth,

00:52:11.178 --> 00:52:13.896
first we're eating like an emperor,

00:52:13.896 --> 00:52:17.404
and then we're taking a journey
to discover the chicken.

00:52:17.404 --> 00:52:18.949
- Food is all about storytelling.

00:52:18.949 --> 00:52:20.617
Well, and taste.

00:52:20.617 --> 00:52:22.824
(Andrew laughs)
But also storytelling.

00:52:22.824 --> 00:52:26.491
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:52:30.335 --> 00:52:32.172
(Steven laughs)

00:52:32.172 --> 00:52:33.150
- Ohh.
- Ohh.

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:34.400
Hey, oh.
- Okay.

00:52:35.691 --> 00:52:36.539
What?

00:52:36.539 --> 00:52:38.140
- Yes.

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:38.973
- What?

00:52:40.207 --> 00:52:41.220
- [Inga] You guys can go up now.

00:52:41.220 --> 00:52:42.353
- Hammer time?
- Okay.

00:52:42.353 --> 00:52:43.367
- All right, Steven.

00:52:43.367 --> 00:52:44.342
I'm nervous.

00:52:44.342 --> 00:52:46.019
- I am scared, too.

00:52:46.019 --> 00:52:47.925
- [Both] One, two, three!

00:52:47.925 --> 00:52:49.156
(clay shatters)

00:52:49.156 --> 00:52:50.708
- Wow.
- Nice!

00:52:50.708 --> 00:52:52.436
- Looks like chocolate.

00:52:52.436 --> 00:52:54.774
- Smells like dirt.
- Dirt.

00:52:54.774 --> 00:52:55.607
(laughs)

00:52:55.607 --> 00:52:57.155
We just hatched our chicken dinner.

00:52:57.155 --> 00:52:59.322
It was very dino egg-like.

00:53:01.011 --> 00:53:02.011
- [Both] Mm.

00:53:04.583 --> 00:53:05.567
- Wow.

00:53:05.567 --> 00:53:06.400
Chicken cheers.

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:07.817
- Chicken cheers.

00:53:09.541 --> 00:53:10.374
- Oh, that's good.

00:53:10.374 --> 00:53:11.243
- What?

00:53:11.243 --> 00:53:12.628
That is so soft.

00:53:12.628 --> 00:53:14.324
- Some people say chicken's a boring meal.

00:53:14.324 --> 00:53:16.464
I would say those people haven't had it--

00:53:16.464 --> 00:53:18.223
- Deep-fried, baked in clay.

00:53:18.223 --> 00:53:20.352
- Stuffed with pork and aromatics.

00:53:20.352 --> 00:53:21.873
- There's pork in the chicken?

00:53:21.873 --> 00:53:22.706
- Yeah.

00:53:22.706 --> 00:53:23.939
- [Inga] So now she's just adding a sauce.

00:53:23.939 --> 00:53:26.939
I think it's an oyster sauce slurry.

00:53:28.283 --> 00:53:29.116
- Oh.

00:53:30.469 --> 00:53:33.697
That tastes like my mom's fried rice.

00:53:33.697 --> 00:53:35.367
Whoa, that was weird.

00:53:35.367 --> 00:53:36.200
- "Mom?"

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:37.177
(laughs)

00:53:37.177 --> 00:53:38.425
You know what this chicken is?

00:53:38.425 --> 00:53:40.332
It's a time capsule.

00:53:40.332 --> 00:53:41.247
For flavor.

00:53:41.247 --> 00:53:43.937
Buried under dirt, much
like a regular time capsule.

00:53:43.937 --> 00:53:46.102
But it's also a dish--

00:53:46.102 --> 00:53:47.151
- From the Ming dynasty.

00:53:47.151 --> 00:53:48.470
Is there a letter written in there?

00:53:48.470 --> 00:53:51.616
Like, "Dear diary, do I still taste good?"

00:53:51.616 --> 00:53:52.449
- Yeah.

00:53:53.938 --> 00:53:54.886
- Very soft.

00:53:54.886 --> 00:53:55.868
- Very soft.

00:53:55.868 --> 00:53:58.477
At its core, this dish is just a plate

00:53:58.477 --> 00:53:59.924
of really nice chicken.

00:53:59.924 --> 00:54:01.643
So simple, so perfect.

00:54:01.643 --> 00:54:04.387
We're going to take it back
into artistry land now,

00:54:04.387 --> 00:54:05.548
if that's okay with you.

00:54:05.548 --> 00:54:06.381
- Let's do it.

00:54:06.381 --> 00:54:08.941
What is going on right now?

00:54:08.941 --> 00:54:10.024
- Yeah, dude.

00:54:10.894 --> 00:54:13.185
(Andrew laughs)

00:54:13.185 --> 00:54:15.419
Looks like a scene from
"Nightmare Before Christmas."

00:54:15.419 --> 00:54:17.038
This is too beautiful not to share.

00:54:17.038 --> 00:54:18.554
I think we should get everybody in here

00:54:18.554 --> 00:54:19.639
and we all take a bite.

00:54:19.639 --> 00:54:22.421
Who wants a swan, who wants a pumpkin?

00:54:22.421 --> 00:54:23.254
Cheers, everybody.

00:54:23.254 --> 00:54:24.837
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:54:27.359 --> 00:54:28.192
- What?

00:54:29.559 --> 00:54:30.712
Oh my god.

00:54:30.712 --> 00:54:31.895
It's red bean.

00:54:31.895 --> 00:54:33.666
- Why?
- Wow.

00:54:33.666 --> 00:54:34.718
- Why?

00:54:34.718 --> 00:54:35.811
- Not "why."

00:54:35.811 --> 00:54:37.373
Why not?

00:54:37.373 --> 00:54:38.326
Worth it.

00:54:38.326 --> 00:54:39.338
Beggar's Chicken.

00:54:39.338 --> 00:54:41.030
You know they say beggars
can't be choosers,

00:54:41.030 --> 00:54:43.083
but beggars can be chicken.

00:54:43.083 --> 00:54:44.649
That was quite an experience.

00:54:44.649 --> 00:54:46.829
Steven, before we get to
our "Worth It" winners,

00:54:46.829 --> 00:54:49.217
what was your favorite
thing that wasn't one

00:54:49.217 --> 00:54:51.724
of the highlight foods in
this "Worth It" episode.

00:54:51.724 --> 00:54:55.037
- The Pork Knuckle at
Chu Zha Xiaoshi Dian.

00:54:55.037 --> 00:54:56.121
Mm.
(hand thuds)

00:54:56.121 --> 00:54:56.954
- That's the knuckle.

00:54:56.954 --> 00:55:00.481
- It was so good, I contemplated
eating my own knuckles.

00:55:00.481 --> 00:55:01.563
What was your favorite thing?

00:55:01.563 --> 00:55:04.630
- Honestly, the hard ass
rain while we were filming

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:05.482
location two.

00:55:05.482 --> 00:55:08.169
We had a typhoon barely
graze us, but we still got

00:55:08.169 --> 00:55:10.046
some awesome hard rain, which I love.

00:55:10.046 --> 00:55:12.392
- Which chicken was the
most worth it to you?

00:55:12.392 --> 00:55:15.315
- My "Worth It" winner,
Walking Su sesame oil chicken.

00:55:15.315 --> 00:55:16.148
- [Steven] Woo.

00:55:16.148 --> 00:55:18.384
- If that chicken had
been in the soup episode,

00:55:18.384 --> 00:55:19.954
it would have won that episode, too.

00:55:19.954 --> 00:55:20.787
- Wow.

00:55:20.787 --> 00:55:23.190
- Comforting, exciting, inexpensive.

00:55:23.190 --> 00:55:24.023
It was wonderful.

00:55:24.023 --> 00:55:24.856
Who was your "Worth It" winner?

00:55:24.856 --> 00:55:25.689
- Okay.

00:55:25.689 --> 00:55:27.173
Walking Su, totally new experience.

00:55:27.173 --> 00:55:28.006
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:55:28.006 --> 00:55:28.839
- Yan Xiang Lou.

00:55:28.839 --> 00:55:30.506
If you could eat with your
eyes, that place would have won.

00:55:30.506 --> 00:55:35.425
But, because that pork
knuckle was so effing good,

00:55:35.425 --> 00:55:39.031
my "Worth It" winner must
go to Chu Zha Xiaoshi Dian.

00:55:39.031 --> 00:55:41.502
- Inga, "Worth It" winner.

00:55:41.502 --> 00:55:43.404
- Two, Chu Zha Xiaoshi Dian.

00:55:43.404 --> 00:55:44.921
With Reebok and Julia.

00:55:44.921 --> 00:55:45.754
- [Steven] Annie?

00:55:45.754 --> 00:55:46.587
- [Annie] The swan.

00:55:46.587 --> 00:55:47.976
- Adam, who's your "Worth It" winner?

00:55:47.976 --> 00:55:49.233
- [Adam] Fried chicken.

00:55:49.233 --> 00:55:50.820
- Oo, the fried chicken.

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:51.739
Very good choice.

00:55:51.739 --> 00:55:52.572
- All right.

00:55:52.572 --> 00:55:54.070
That's a wrap on "Worth It: Taiwan."

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:56.711
- Very special thank you to
the Taiwan Tourism Bureau,

00:55:56.711 --> 00:55:59.185
that helped us out
tremendously with this trip.

00:55:59.185 --> 00:56:00.314
Couldn't have been here without them.

00:56:00.314 --> 00:56:02.415
- All right, let's get out of here.

00:56:02.415 --> 00:56:03.248
Bye!

00:56:04.231 --> 00:56:07.398
(upbeat groovy music)

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:17.033
- Welcome back.

00:56:17.033 --> 00:56:18.197
- Welcome back, Steven.

00:56:18.197 --> 00:56:20.195
We're doing another three episode burst

00:56:20.195 --> 00:56:22.267
before the new year rolls in.

00:56:22.267 --> 00:56:24.294
You might be wondering
why we're not driving.

00:56:24.294 --> 00:56:25.185
Say hi, Devon.

00:56:25.185 --> 00:56:26.491
Devon's up front driving

00:56:26.491 --> 00:56:28.942
because we're picking up
our special co-star today.

00:56:28.942 --> 00:56:32.401
He's an avid fan of the show,
from River Forest, Illinois.

00:56:32.401 --> 00:56:34.548
His name is Jackson, and
his visit is made possible

00:56:34.548 --> 00:56:36.577
by Make-A-Wish.

00:56:36.577 --> 00:56:37.732
- [Steven] What's up?

00:56:37.732 --> 00:56:38.787
Nice to meet you

00:56:38.787 --> 00:56:39.620
(parents laughing)

00:56:39.620 --> 00:56:40.967
Hi, guys!
- Hey.

00:56:40.967 --> 00:56:41.934
- You ready to go?
- Yes.

00:56:41.934 --> 00:56:43.492
- [Steven] All right.

00:56:43.492 --> 00:56:44.325
(grunts)

00:56:44.325 --> 00:56:45.745
There's Adam, say hi.

00:56:45.745 --> 00:56:48.677
- Hey.
(Andrew laughs)

00:56:48.677 --> 00:56:49.974
- Welcome to the car, Jackson.

00:56:49.974 --> 00:56:51.156
- It's the worth automobile.

00:56:51.156 --> 00:56:52.261
- That's right.
- Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:56:52.261 --> 00:56:54.410
I'm the pun master on this show.

00:56:54.410 --> 00:56:55.243
- [Steven] I like it.

00:56:55.243 --> 00:56:56.451
- But thank you Jackson for joining us

00:56:56.451 --> 00:56:58.840
on our double cheeseburger episode today.

00:56:58.840 --> 00:56:59.886
- Thank you for having me.

00:56:59.886 --> 00:57:02.299
- You flew all the way out from Illinois.

00:57:02.299 --> 00:57:03.562
- Yes.
- The Midwest,

00:57:03.562 --> 00:57:04.569
I like you already.

00:57:04.569 --> 00:57:06.311
- Should we do the Today on Worth It?

00:57:06.311 --> 00:57:07.166
- You don't know how to do that?

00:57:07.166 --> 00:57:08.934
- Today on "Worth It", we're having

00:57:08.934 --> 00:57:11.245
three double cheeseburgers
at three addresses

00:57:11.245 --> 00:57:12.405
at three different price points

00:57:12.405 --> 00:57:13.704
to see which one is worth it.

00:57:13.704 --> 00:57:14.596
- The most worth it.

00:57:14.596 --> 00:57:15.535
- [Together] The most worth it.

00:57:15.535 --> 00:57:17.251
- At its given price point.

00:57:17.251 --> 00:57:19.532
- So that's at least six patties.

00:57:19.532 --> 00:57:20.557
- Are you prepared?

00:57:20.557 --> 00:57:21.513
- I'm hungry.

00:57:21.513 --> 00:57:22.346
- Good

00:57:22.346 --> 00:57:23.179
- So the first place we're going to

00:57:23.179 --> 00:57:24.987
is called Burgers Never Say Die.

00:57:24.987 --> 00:57:26.415
We're gonna see Shawn.

00:57:26.415 --> 00:57:27.936
- This is a smash burger joint.

00:57:27.936 --> 00:57:31.318
I love Super Smash, the
game, Ultimate, and all.

00:57:31.318 --> 00:57:32.190
You play Ultimate?

00:57:32.190 --> 00:57:33.023
- Yes

00:57:33.023 --> 00:57:34.890
- Ooh, who's your main?

00:57:34.890 --> 00:57:36.326
- (chuckling) Oh my God.

00:57:36.326 --> 00:57:37.271
- Donkey Kong.

00:57:37.271 --> 00:57:39.057
- Okay, okay, I'm a Ness man.

00:57:39.057 --> 00:57:40.974
- You're bringing all
the co-host attributes

00:57:40.974 --> 00:57:44.099
that Steven wants in
me, but I don't possess.

00:57:44.099 --> 00:57:45.754
- Hey, do you watch the NBA?

00:57:45.754 --> 00:57:46.587
- Yes.

00:57:46.587 --> 00:57:47.889
(all exclaiming)

00:57:47.889 --> 00:57:50.556
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:57:51.552 --> 00:57:53.987
- So your menu starts with the regular,

00:57:53.987 --> 00:57:55.672
and the regular has two patties.

00:57:55.672 --> 00:57:56.675
- Yes
- Why is that?

00:57:56.675 --> 00:57:57.859
- I like double cheeseburgers.

00:57:57.859 --> 00:57:59.008
That's what I grew up eating.

00:57:59.008 --> 00:58:00.956
It also works because it's a smash patty.

00:58:00.956 --> 00:58:02.850
- [Andrew] And tell us
what a smash burger is?

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:05.087
- [Shawn] It's cooked on a
flat top, at very high heat,

00:58:05.087 --> 00:58:07.693
and you just smash it down
there, so it sears to the griddle

00:58:07.693 --> 00:58:10.094
and the whole process probably
takes a minute or less.

00:58:10.094 --> 00:58:11.386
- [Jackson] What makes
a really good burger?

00:58:11.386 --> 00:58:13.573
- [Shawn] It's so subjective,
like personally, for me,

00:58:13.573 --> 00:58:15.743
I like burgers cooked on the griddle.

00:58:15.743 --> 00:58:16.864
- [Jackson] What does the griddle add?

00:58:16.864 --> 00:58:18.720
- [Shawn] You get flavor that's
been there, the seasoning

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:20.251
from the food items other than burgers.

00:58:20.251 --> 00:58:22.485
- [Andrew] What drew you to
making burgers specifically?

00:58:22.485 --> 00:58:24.466
- My wife and I, we were
always really into burgers

00:58:24.466 --> 00:58:26.330
and got into the food scene of LA.

00:58:26.330 --> 00:58:27.568
Bringing together all the burgers we ate,

00:58:27.568 --> 00:58:29.763
I thought, I wanna try
making this burger myself.

00:58:29.763 --> 00:58:30.889
I started in my backyard

00:58:30.889 --> 00:58:32.663
with no intention of opening a restaurant.

00:58:32.663 --> 00:58:34.212
For a couple weeks, I was
just begging people to come

00:58:34.212 --> 00:58:36.484
and try the burger;
friends, coworkers, whoever.

00:58:36.484 --> 00:58:37.951
And then, some people approached me,

00:58:37.951 --> 00:58:39.602
asking if I wanted to open a restaurant.

00:58:39.602 --> 00:58:40.657
And throughout my whole life,

00:58:40.657 --> 00:58:41.795
I usually just jump in,

00:58:41.795 --> 00:58:43.827
and I thought, let's give this
a shot and see what happens.

00:58:43.827 --> 00:58:46.713
- We have a sign right here
that says, "always order two".

00:58:46.713 --> 00:58:47.546
Why?

00:58:47.546 --> 00:58:48.785
- It's an ode to the backyard.

00:58:48.785 --> 00:58:51.236
People would order one,
and they would regret it

00:58:51.236 --> 00:58:53.560
and then there would be a long
line or we had sold out, so.

00:58:53.560 --> 00:58:55.024
- Is two still doable?

00:58:55.024 --> 00:58:56.472
- I recommend two for you, yeah.

00:58:56.472 --> 00:58:57.701
- What about you, do you do two?

00:58:57.701 --> 00:59:00.044
- I do two, I've eaten
like six in one day, so.

00:59:00.044 --> 00:59:01.546
(laughing)
- Oh my goodness.

00:59:01.546 --> 00:59:03.403
- I mean we're gonna do six probably,

00:59:03.403 --> 00:59:05.395
over the course of this day, for us,

00:59:05.395 --> 00:59:06.973
so why not start with two?

00:59:06.973 --> 00:59:08.794
- (sighs) Here we go.

00:59:08.794 --> 00:59:10.295
(cash register dings)

00:59:10.295 --> 00:59:11.573
- [Andrew] All right,
Jackson, take it away.

00:59:11.573 --> 00:59:13.820
- Worth-it, Double Cheeseburger, take one.

00:59:13.820 --> 00:59:14.653
- Nice.
- That was perfect.

00:59:14.653 --> 00:59:15.882
That was better than I usually do.

00:59:15.882 --> 00:59:16.715
Take two.

00:59:16.715 --> 00:59:19.034
(clattering) (laughing)

00:59:19.034 --> 00:59:20.225
So that Burgers Never Say Die,

00:59:20.225 --> 00:59:21.980
they also have soft serve ice cream.

00:59:21.980 --> 00:59:23.193
Insider pro tip;

00:59:23.193 --> 00:59:25.940
you get your soft serve in a
cup, order a soda on the side,

00:59:25.940 --> 00:59:27.946
and then mix it for your own float.

00:59:27.946 --> 00:59:30.061
What are the correct
proportions for a float?

00:59:30.061 --> 00:59:30.926
Does anybody know?

00:59:30.926 --> 00:59:32.105
- The whole bottle.
- It's a feel.

00:59:32.105 --> 00:59:34.269
- Jackson's got classic root beer float.

00:59:34.269 --> 00:59:35.340
This is Steven's--

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:36.665
- [Steven] I got chocolate--

00:59:36.665 --> 00:59:38.697
- And a blueberry root beer.

00:59:38.697 --> 00:59:39.778
Okay, when, when, when, when.

00:59:39.778 --> 00:59:42.018
Cheers!
- Cheers!

00:59:42.018 --> 00:59:43.331
How do you eat this?

00:59:43.331 --> 00:59:44.892
- Who thought of floats?
- Ooh!

00:59:44.892 --> 00:59:46.832
- Pour some soda on some ice cream?

00:59:46.832 --> 00:59:47.665
That's like some,

00:59:47.665 --> 00:59:50.321
the-parents-aren't-home,
mischief (all laughing).

00:59:50.321 --> 00:59:51.571
- [All] Cheers.

00:59:52.956 --> 00:59:53.821
- The fries!

00:59:53.821 --> 00:59:55.960
- Taste like McDonald's.
- Taste like McDonald's, yeah.

00:59:55.960 --> 00:59:58.614
- So these fries are fried in beef tallow.

00:59:58.614 --> 01:00:00.790
Apparently this is what
McDonald's tasted like

01:00:00.790 --> 01:00:01.910
way back in the day,

01:00:01.910 --> 01:00:04.303
before they switched to a vegetable oil.

01:00:04.303 --> 01:00:05.933
Staying true to their motto,

01:00:05.933 --> 01:00:09.049
we've two burgers for each
of us except for you Jackson,

01:00:09.049 --> 01:00:11.562
because this is your first
time on one of these rodeos,

01:00:11.562 --> 01:00:13.402
I don't wanna do that to you.

01:00:13.402 --> 01:00:14.694
- Whoa.

01:00:14.694 --> 01:00:18.300
- It looks the way a fast food
burger looks in a commercial,

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:19.974
but this is how it is in real life.

01:00:19.974 --> 01:00:21.870
- [Jackson] It smells good.

01:00:21.870 --> 01:00:24.015
- Great bun smell.

01:00:24.015 --> 01:00:25.461
Did you think you'd have to wait this long

01:00:25.461 --> 01:00:27.063
before you actually get to take a bite?

01:00:27.063 --> 01:00:29.186
- Can I point out one
more physical attribute

01:00:29.186 --> 01:00:31.283
of this burger before we bite into it?

01:00:31.283 --> 01:00:32.127
Sorry, Jackson.

01:00:32.127 --> 01:00:34.157
Look at the way it's like a lattice.

01:00:34.157 --> 01:00:35.151
- [Andrew] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:00:35.151 --> 01:00:36.631
- Crispy?
- Very crispy.

01:00:36.631 --> 01:00:38.681
But you can almost see through the meat.

01:00:38.681 --> 01:00:39.860
- [Andrew] Like a spider's web.

01:00:39.860 --> 01:00:41.110
- [All] Cheers!

01:00:43.794 --> 01:00:45.155
- Oh, man.

01:00:45.155 --> 01:00:45.988
Wow!

01:00:45.988 --> 01:00:46.950
- It's crunchy too

01:00:46.950 --> 01:00:48.885
- It's really crunchy.
- Yeah.

01:00:48.885 --> 01:00:50.372
- It's the essence of burger.

01:00:50.372 --> 01:00:51.205
There's nothing extra.

01:00:51.205 --> 01:00:54.237
There's not like strange
sauces or condiments.

01:00:54.237 --> 01:00:55.070
- Oh man.

01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:56.216
- This might be the best
burger I've ever had.

01:00:56.216 --> 01:00:57.049
- Oh man.

01:00:57.049 --> 01:00:57.882
- Like truly.

01:00:57.882 --> 01:00:58.715
- Would you agree with that?

01:00:58.715 --> 01:00:59.548
- Yes.

01:00:59.548 --> 01:01:00.858
- This is one of my favorite ones.

01:01:00.858 --> 01:01:02.047
- Swallow your food.

01:01:02.047 --> 01:01:03.136
(Jackson and Steven laughing)

01:01:03.136 --> 01:01:04.784
- Next burger, here we go.

01:01:04.784 --> 01:01:07.435
- You would definitely be
satisfied by one of these,

01:01:07.435 --> 01:01:08.748
but you're gonna want two.

01:01:08.748 --> 01:01:09.642
- I do agree.

01:01:09.642 --> 01:01:10.475
Order two.

01:01:10.475 --> 01:01:12.497
What am I doing with my life now?

01:01:12.497 --> 01:01:14.496
I'm only slightly lactose-intolerant,

01:01:14.496 --> 01:01:16.673
so low levels of cheese, that's fine.

01:01:16.673 --> 01:01:18.015
Are you lactose-intolerant at all?

01:01:18.015 --> 01:01:18.848
- No.

01:01:18.848 --> 01:01:19.681
- You're a lucky person.

01:01:19.681 --> 01:01:20.680
Oh, we had ice cream, too.

01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:22.684
What am I doing? (all laughing)

01:01:22.684 --> 01:01:23.979
- [Andrew] Jackson, what do you think

01:01:23.979 --> 01:01:25.715
about the part of the
show where Adam eats?

01:01:25.715 --> 01:01:27.512
- He takes really large bites.

01:01:27.512 --> 01:01:29.550
(laughing)

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:30.936
- I'm gonna finish mine before you.

01:01:30.936 --> 01:01:31.936
- Of course.

01:01:33.801 --> 01:01:34.943
I don't know if I could do that.

01:01:34.943 --> 01:01:35.783
- What a cheeseburger.

01:01:35.783 --> 01:01:36.885
- [Jackson and Steven] That was insane.

01:01:36.885 --> 01:01:38.983
- We keep talking at the same time.

01:01:38.983 --> 01:01:40.042
- Oh.

01:01:40.042 --> 01:01:41.771
- Twins!
- Look who is simpatico.

01:01:41.771 --> 01:01:44.335
So before our next double cheeseburger,

01:01:44.335 --> 01:01:46.510
we're actually gonna get
another double cheeseburger.

01:01:46.510 --> 01:01:47.873
We're going to this French restaurant

01:01:47.873 --> 01:01:49.479
in West Hollywood, called Tesse.

01:01:49.479 --> 01:01:52.783
I saw on Instagram, that
for National Burger day,

01:01:52.783 --> 01:01:53.876
they made, we're just gonna call it

01:01:53.876 --> 01:01:55.311
a special dessert burger.

01:01:55.311 --> 01:01:58.019
We reached out, and
the pastry chef, Sally,

01:01:58.019 --> 01:02:01.368
so kindly agreed to remake
it, but double this time.

01:02:01.368 --> 01:02:02.792
This might be the most visually

01:02:02.792 --> 01:02:04.592
appealing burger of the day.

01:02:04.592 --> 01:02:07.651
(charming old school music)

01:02:07.651 --> 01:02:09.167
- Close your eyes.
- Okay.

01:02:09.167 --> 01:02:10.657
- Man, this is gonna blow you away.

01:02:10.657 --> 01:02:12.898
Three, two, one, reveal!

01:02:12.898 --> 01:02:14.492
(Steven and Jackson exclaiming)

01:02:14.492 --> 01:02:15.615
- [Andrew] It's very realistic.

01:02:15.615 --> 01:02:17.921
- Dude, is that a strawberry?

01:02:17.921 --> 01:02:18.768
- [Jackson and Andrew] Yes.

01:02:18.768 --> 01:02:20.520
- [Andrew] Two macaroons,
chocolate ganache,

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:24.459
mango passion fruit cheese,
strawberry slices, shiso leaf,

01:02:24.459 --> 01:02:26.431
and a raspberry jelly for the ketchup.

01:02:26.431 --> 01:02:27.264
- Wow.

01:02:27.264 --> 01:02:28.900
If you did not tell me that was dessert,

01:02:28.900 --> 01:02:30.197
I would not have guessed it.

01:02:30.197 --> 01:02:31.447
- [All] Cheers!

01:02:32.415 --> 01:02:34.169
- [Steven] How to eat this?

01:02:34.169 --> 01:02:36.419
(mumbling)

01:02:38.536 --> 01:02:40.007
- It's actually really good.

01:02:40.007 --> 01:02:42.172
I mean it's chocolate and
fruit, what's not to like?

01:02:42.172 --> 01:02:44.016
- It's sweet and bitter at the same time.

01:02:44.016 --> 01:02:44.976
- [Steven] That's the nosh.

01:02:44.976 --> 01:02:47.048
- The tasting notes coming off of Jackson.

01:02:47.048 --> 01:02:48.498
This isn't your first time, is it?

01:02:48.498 --> 01:02:50.153
- No.
- All right, I retire today,

01:02:50.153 --> 01:02:51.363
you can have the--

01:02:51.363 --> 01:02:52.576
- Do you know what time it is now?

01:02:52.576 --> 01:02:53.569
- Yes.

01:02:53.569 --> 01:02:55.054
Oh, you're asking me or Jackson?

01:02:55.054 --> 01:02:57.023
- If you didn't know, I'd be concerned.

01:02:57.023 --> 01:02:57.856
- Fact time.

01:02:57.856 --> 01:02:59.577
- That's right, double cheeseburger fact.

01:02:59.577 --> 01:03:01.875
- Double cheeseburger fact.

01:03:01.875 --> 01:03:02.781
- One minute.

01:03:02.781 --> 01:03:04.012
(all laughing)

01:03:04.012 --> 01:03:05.171
Double cheeseburger fact.

01:03:05.171 --> 01:03:06.642
- Bob's Big Boy, is credited with selling

01:03:06.642 --> 01:03:09.340
the first double cheeseburger in 1937.

01:03:09.340 --> 01:03:12.632
The chain's original name was Bob's Pantry

01:03:12.632 --> 01:03:14.174
but after the double cheeseburger,

01:03:14.174 --> 01:03:16.450
dubbed the Big Boy, was invented,

01:03:16.450 --> 01:03:20.078
the restaurant changed its name
to boast its famous burger.

01:03:20.078 --> 01:03:22.326
The double cheeseburger became so famous,

01:03:22.326 --> 01:03:23.847
they changed their restaurant name.

01:03:23.847 --> 01:03:25.391
- [Adam] What about renaming this show?

01:03:25.391 --> 01:03:27.191
- Whoa.
- Whoa.

01:03:27.191 --> 01:03:29.700
It would probably be
called, Adam's Silent Bite.

01:03:29.700 --> 01:03:30.618
- No, Adam's Big Bite.

01:03:30.618 --> 01:03:31.564
- Adam's Big Bite.

01:03:31.564 --> 01:03:32.397
Yes!

01:03:32.397 --> 01:03:33.230
For our next double cheeseburger,

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:35.431
we're on our way to see Frederick and Max,

01:03:35.431 --> 01:03:37.763
at Burgerlords, in Highland Park.

01:03:37.763 --> 01:03:39.319
Similar to this double cheeseburger,

01:03:39.319 --> 01:03:42.968
this next double cheeseburger's
made without meat.

01:03:42.968 --> 01:03:45.359
- I've never had a veggie
cheeseburger before.

01:03:45.359 --> 01:03:46.786
- Curveball for the episode.

01:03:46.786 --> 01:03:48.447
- I'm scared and excited.

01:03:48.447 --> 01:03:52.852
- Scared and excited is a
good place to be with food.

01:03:52.852 --> 01:03:55.064
Tell us what kind of
restaurant Burgerlords is.

01:03:55.064 --> 01:03:57.441
- The way I think of it, it's
kind of a bizzaro version

01:03:57.441 --> 01:03:59.449
of a traditional fast food restaurant.

01:03:59.449 --> 01:04:02.361
You're used to a frozen
garden burger, portobello.

01:04:02.361 --> 01:04:05.355
This is the opposite, where
everything is vegan, vegetarian,

01:04:05.355 --> 01:04:06.715
except the beef burger.

01:04:06.715 --> 01:04:08.211
- Do you think you're more popular

01:04:08.211 --> 01:04:09.872
with traditional beef cheeseburgers

01:04:09.872 --> 01:04:12.460
or with the vegetarian and vegan crowd?

01:04:12.460 --> 01:04:14.467
- Honestly it's like 50-50.

01:04:14.467 --> 01:04:15.825
- Yeah?
- Yeah we look at it

01:04:15.825 --> 01:04:16.658
every week.

01:04:16.658 --> 01:04:17.587
- [Steven] How did you guys develop

01:04:17.587 --> 01:04:19.798
the recipe for this patty?

01:04:19.798 --> 01:04:21.660
- [Max] Everything kind of has its role.

01:04:21.660 --> 01:04:25.223
The barley for chewiness, and
cashews give it a nice crunch.

01:04:25.223 --> 01:04:27.121
We're roasting all the eggplants,

01:04:27.121 --> 01:04:28.856
slicing up the other vegetables.

01:04:28.856 --> 01:04:32.118
Those get sauteed with all
the spices on the flat top.

01:04:32.118 --> 01:04:35.571
Cooking barley in a rice
cooker, with kambu and miso,

01:04:35.571 --> 01:04:38.584
roasting nuts, garbanzo beans, cashews,

01:04:38.584 --> 01:04:42.028
and then it all goes into
one batch, and just mixed up.

01:04:42.028 --> 01:04:43.548
- [Fred] Once it's cold, it settles,

01:04:43.548 --> 01:04:45.940
and then we're balling it
like a traditional burger.

01:04:45.940 --> 01:04:48.142
We roll it in Panko, then we patty it out,

01:04:48.142 --> 01:04:51.562
so the Panko when it's cooking
gives it a kinda crispy edge.

01:04:51.562 --> 01:04:54.096
- [Max] Helps keep the patty
from burning on the grill, too.

01:04:54.096 --> 01:04:55.926
- [Fred] All of the work
comes at the beginning,

01:04:55.926 --> 01:04:58.278
so we can push it out really fast.

01:04:58.278 --> 01:05:01.208
On average, I mean our tickets,
are like five to 10 minutes.

01:05:01.208 --> 01:05:02.637
It's like neo-fast food.

01:05:02.637 --> 01:05:04.789
- [Max] It feels like fast
food, it looks like it

01:05:04.789 --> 01:05:08.154
on the exterior, but in the
kitchen, we're cooking all day.

01:05:08.154 --> 01:05:09.788
- [Steven] Now that you've
explained all of this,

01:05:09.788 --> 01:05:11.593
it feels like a burger joint

01:05:11.593 --> 01:05:14.730
is the hardest thing
to do for vegetarians.

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:17.544
Like why did you wanna
tackle a burger joint?

01:05:17.544 --> 01:05:19.068
- You know, there's the nostalgia.

01:05:19.068 --> 01:05:20.986
You know, it's the experience of a diner,

01:05:20.986 --> 01:05:24.391
a fast food restaurant, having
fries and eating a burger.

01:05:24.391 --> 01:05:27.059
It was also to create
something that we didn't think

01:05:27.059 --> 01:05:28.305
really existed here.

01:05:28.305 --> 01:05:29.449
You know, when we grew up,

01:05:29.449 --> 01:05:31.216
there was a lot of times we felt left out,

01:05:31.216 --> 01:05:33.703
so we wanted to make
sure that everyone felt

01:05:33.703 --> 01:05:35.305
they had a good option here.

01:05:35.305 --> 01:05:36.848
(cash register dings)

01:05:36.848 --> 01:05:38.310
- So to round out our experience,

01:05:38.310 --> 01:05:41.714
we also got the tofu
nuggets and french fries,

01:05:41.714 --> 01:05:43.360
lord-of-the-fries style.

01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:45.592
- Lord of the fries, that's a good pun.

01:05:45.592 --> 01:05:48.759
Have you read "Lord of the Fries" yet?

01:05:49.727 --> 01:05:51.607
(all laughing)
- Oh God.

01:05:51.607 --> 01:05:52.440
- We can't take you anywhere.

01:05:52.440 --> 01:05:54.745
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

01:05:54.745 --> 01:05:55.578
- Ah, this is good.

01:05:55.578 --> 01:05:57.431
We need napkins.
- Oh my goodness.

01:05:57.431 --> 01:05:59.165
For the first time in my life,

01:05:59.165 --> 01:06:01.425
I have somebody who is also a messy eater.

01:06:01.425 --> 01:06:02.258
Look at that.

01:06:02.258 --> 01:06:03.091
- [Jackson] Cheese crust.

01:06:03.091 --> 01:06:04.352
- [Andrew] It's got the cheese crust.

01:06:04.352 --> 01:06:05.989
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

01:06:05.989 --> 01:06:07.989
- [Steven] Veggie bumps.

01:06:09.552 --> 01:06:11.539
- This is good.
- Wow.

01:06:11.539 --> 01:06:12.910
- It doesn't taste like plants at all.

01:06:12.910 --> 01:06:14.013
It tastes like real beef.

01:06:14.013 --> 01:06:18.154
- It's hot, cheesy, juicy,
meaty, and yet there's no meat.

01:06:18.154 --> 01:06:19.699
So when you think, "Man, I could really go

01:06:19.699 --> 01:06:21.279
"for a burger right now."

01:06:21.279 --> 01:06:23.590
Does this satisfy that feeling?

01:06:23.590 --> 01:06:24.507
- 100%

01:06:25.637 --> 01:06:27.887
(laughing)

01:06:29.395 --> 01:06:30.807
- Are you gonna finish that thing Steven?

01:06:30.807 --> 01:06:31.640
- I'm trying to.

01:06:31.640 --> 01:06:32.673
- Put a little hot sauce on there.

01:06:32.673 --> 01:06:33.531
- Ooh!
- Ooh!

01:06:33.531 --> 01:06:36.003
- Yeah, hot sauce me.

01:06:36.003 --> 01:06:37.526
You know what, that's really good.

01:06:37.526 --> 01:06:38.394
It's a nice little kick.

01:06:38.394 --> 01:06:40.951
It really brightens the
flavor of the burger.

01:06:40.951 --> 01:06:43.581
- Oh, it has a good
kick to it, that's good.

01:06:43.581 --> 01:06:46.053
- It is like I'm in a
different universe right now.

01:06:46.053 --> 01:06:49.673
Vegetarian food, in this
universe, is the norm.

01:06:49.673 --> 01:06:52.460
- It does kind a feel like
we're in a Twilight Zone set,

01:06:52.460 --> 01:06:54.577
a little bit, just like
transported back in time,

01:06:54.577 --> 01:06:57.454
but to an alternate reality
where everybody's a vegetarian,

01:06:57.454 --> 01:06:58.583
and you know what?

01:06:58.583 --> 01:06:59.416
Pretty good.

01:06:59.416 --> 01:07:01.833
You ever watch Twilight Zone?

01:07:02.896 --> 01:07:04.724
- [Andrew] Wow, that was a good bite.

01:07:04.724 --> 01:07:06.698
- This is so good.

01:07:06.698 --> 01:07:07.531
- Life blowing.

01:07:07.531 --> 01:07:08.512
- Life blowing (laughing)

01:07:08.512 --> 01:07:10.113
- Not just mind blowing

01:07:10.113 --> 01:07:10.946
- Life blowing

01:07:10.946 --> 01:07:11.822
- Life.

01:07:11.822 --> 01:07:14.288
Oh my gosh, guys, I'm on patty number ten.

01:07:14.288 --> 01:07:15.633
(laughing)

01:07:15.633 --> 01:07:16.578
- That was eight.
- Oh, sorry, eight.

01:07:16.578 --> 01:07:17.411
- It was eight.

01:07:17.411 --> 01:07:18.244
Yeah.
- I can't count anymore.

01:07:18.244 --> 01:07:19.420
That's how many patties I've had.

01:07:19.420 --> 01:07:22.173
- Before we go to our last
burger, it's time for one more--

01:07:22.173 --> 01:07:23.060
- Burger fact.

01:07:23.060 --> 01:07:24.403
- [Andrew] Double cheeseburger fact!

01:07:24.403 --> 01:07:26.241
- Oh dang it. (laughs)

01:07:26.241 --> 01:07:27.429
- Would you like to read the fact today?

01:07:27.429 --> 01:07:28.262
- Sure.

01:07:28.262 --> 01:07:29.224
The price of the Big Mac

01:07:29.224 --> 01:07:32.142
when it debuted nationwide was 49 cents.

01:07:32.142 --> 01:07:33.763
- 49 cents?

01:07:33.763 --> 01:07:35.198
So you're telling me I
could go to a McDonald's

01:07:35.198 --> 01:07:37.667
with two quarters and come back
with a Big Mac and a penny?

01:07:37.667 --> 01:07:39.748
- Yeah, in the 60s, when it debuted.

01:07:39.748 --> 01:07:40.581
- Oh, sorry.

01:07:40.581 --> 01:07:41.569
- [Adam] That happened a long--

01:07:41.569 --> 01:07:42.402
- Back in the 60s.

01:07:42.402 --> 01:07:43.235
- Back in the 60s, right.

01:07:43.235 --> 01:07:44.746
- We're on our way to Petit Trois,

01:07:44.746 --> 01:07:46.448
where we're gonna see Chef Ludo,

01:07:46.448 --> 01:07:49.989
and his double cheeseburger
called, The Big Mec.

01:07:49.989 --> 01:07:51.445
- And what is on this Big Mec?

01:07:51.445 --> 01:07:55.028
- Well, it's a French
Restaurant, so sauce.

01:07:56.812 --> 01:07:59.451
- Petit Trois, it's a
classic French restaurant.

01:07:59.451 --> 01:08:02.001
The design and the food is very French.

01:08:02.001 --> 01:08:03.217
- And so you have this burger.

01:08:03.217 --> 01:08:05.448
How does that fit into that classic menu?

01:08:05.448 --> 01:08:07.462
- In France, we eat a lot of steak hache,

01:08:07.462 --> 01:08:10.212
you know, is a patty, so I
decided to just do a burger,

01:08:10.212 --> 01:08:11.705
like what Americans like, you know?

01:08:11.705 --> 01:08:13.257
And look like a burger, too.

01:08:13.257 --> 01:08:14.743
- [Jackson] What makes
a really good burger?

01:08:14.743 --> 01:08:17.093
- [Ludo] A burger needs
to be crispy and juicy.

01:08:17.093 --> 01:08:19.491
When I eat a steak or any
meat, that's what I want.

01:08:19.491 --> 01:08:21.023
I want the meat to be crispy

01:08:21.023 --> 01:08:24.002
- It's called the Big Mec,
styled after the Big Mac,

01:08:24.002 --> 01:08:24.835
of course, right?

01:08:24.835 --> 01:08:25.944
- Of course, yeah yeah, of course.

01:08:25.944 --> 01:08:28.077
Mec, in France, means guys,

01:08:28.077 --> 01:08:32.442
(speaks in foreign language)

01:08:32.442 --> 01:08:33.975
French food is all about the sauce,

01:08:33.975 --> 01:08:36.143
so what's very important
for me to have my burger

01:08:36.143 --> 01:08:38.094
in the plate, swimming in the sauce,

01:08:38.094 --> 01:08:40.187
like when you eat a
steak, so I decided to do

01:08:40.187 --> 01:08:42.810
a classic French sauce, called Bordelaise,

01:08:42.810 --> 01:08:45.958
beef stock with a lot of
shallot and a red wine sauce.

01:08:45.958 --> 01:08:47.417
We take like eight hour to do,

01:08:47.417 --> 01:08:49.786
then after we have aged cheddar cheese.

01:08:49.786 --> 01:08:51.900
How do you know if I'm going
to put aged cheddar cheese

01:08:51.900 --> 01:08:53.014
or American cheese?

01:08:53.014 --> 01:08:54.163
Because I love American cheese.

01:08:54.163 --> 01:08:55.184
- Really?
- Oh I love it.

01:08:55.184 --> 01:08:56.017
- You love American cheese?

01:08:56.017 --> 01:08:56.850
- Oh we don't like American cheese?

01:08:56.850 --> 01:08:58.019
Guys, come on.
- Some people don't.

01:08:58.019 --> 01:09:00.805
- When I think about French
cuisine, I think fine dining,

01:09:00.805 --> 01:09:02.507
or I think like kind of--

01:09:02.507 --> 01:09:03.340
- Foufou?

01:09:03.340 --> 01:09:04.387
- [Steven] Yeah, a little bit,

01:09:04.387 --> 01:09:07.466
I mean this burger is not
what comes to mind, right?

01:09:07.466 --> 01:09:10.954
- My job as a chef is also
to cook what people want.

01:09:10.954 --> 01:09:14.766
And it's fun to mix American
food with French cuisine.

01:09:14.766 --> 01:09:17.455
I recommend to eat the
burger with a fork and knife.

01:09:17.455 --> 01:09:18.900
Very French foufou, like you say.

01:09:18.900 --> 01:09:21.182
- You said that, not me.

01:09:21.182 --> 01:09:22.298
- So it is fork and knife?

01:09:22.298 --> 01:09:24.660
- In France, we eat our
burger with a fork and knife.

01:09:24.660 --> 01:09:26.657
Okay guys, sorry, yeah we do.

01:09:26.657 --> 01:09:27.940
(cash register dings)

01:09:27.940 --> 01:09:28.773
- [Steven] There it is.

01:09:28.773 --> 01:09:31.417
- It's really unlike any
burger I've ever seen before.

01:09:31.417 --> 01:09:32.702
- Look at how it's plated.

01:09:32.702 --> 01:09:33.906
- [Jackson] It's in a pool of sauce.

01:09:33.906 --> 01:09:36.612
- Yeah, it looks like a
lily pad in a murky pond.

01:09:36.612 --> 01:09:37.681
- [Steven] Will you do the honors?

01:09:37.681 --> 01:09:38.631
- Oh man, oh okay.

01:09:38.631 --> 01:09:39.697
- [Steven] Everybody on the internet

01:09:39.697 --> 01:09:40.907
is going to watch you cut this in half.

01:09:40.907 --> 01:09:42.513
- We're losing traction here.

01:09:42.513 --> 01:09:43.746
- Oh, god. (laughs)

01:09:43.746 --> 01:09:45.169
- [Andrew] This is a $25 cheeseburger

01:09:45.169 --> 01:09:46.224
that you're just hacking through.

01:09:46.224 --> 01:09:47.123
- [Steven] Double.

01:09:47.123 --> 01:09:49.499
- Stab through the whole
thing to secure your bite.

01:09:49.499 --> 01:09:50.332
What?

01:09:50.332 --> 01:09:51.792
Oh god. (all laughing)

01:09:51.792 --> 01:09:54.863
- I regret saying that I would
be okay sharing a burger.

01:09:54.863 --> 01:09:56.566
I'm gonna cheers?
- Cheers!

01:09:56.566 --> 01:09:59.816
- And then I'm gonna mop up some sauce.

01:10:00.859 --> 01:10:01.841
- It's magical.

01:10:01.841 --> 01:10:02.743
- Yeah.
- Yeah, boy.

01:10:02.743 --> 01:10:03.576
- Wow.

01:10:03.576 --> 01:10:05.097
Oh man, that sauce.

01:10:05.097 --> 01:10:06.478
- That sauce.
- Is incredible.

01:10:06.478 --> 01:10:08.701
- The patty is so crispy.

01:10:08.701 --> 01:10:11.338
- It's juicy and crispy at the same time.

01:10:11.338 --> 01:10:13.489
- Okay, if I were to
go back to Burgerlords,

01:10:13.489 --> 01:10:15.800
that was everything I want in a burger,

01:10:15.800 --> 01:10:16.664
but it's vegetarian.

01:10:16.664 --> 01:10:18.852
- This is everything I didn't
know I wanted in a burger.

01:10:18.852 --> 01:10:21.804
- Exactly, I don't think I've
had a sauce like this before,

01:10:21.804 --> 01:10:25.290
that is so rich and yet tantalizing.

01:10:25.290 --> 01:10:28.763
- Yes, the sourness just
makes you crave more.

01:10:28.763 --> 01:10:30.087
- Yes, it does.

01:10:30.087 --> 01:10:31.234
- Jackson's like, keep talking

01:10:31.234 --> 01:10:32.618
while I eat the rest of this burger.

01:10:32.618 --> 01:10:33.910
- (chuckling) He is destroying our burger.

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:35.981
- Is this what you thought
an expensive burger

01:10:35.981 --> 01:10:37.636
would look like on Worth It?

01:10:37.636 --> 01:10:39.297
- No, I thought it would look fancier.

01:10:39.297 --> 01:10:42.404
Most expensive burgers, like
your episode with Keith,

01:10:42.404 --> 01:10:45.182
it was a lot of ingredients,
like foie gras and lobster.

01:10:45.182 --> 01:10:48.790
This one just has beef
and cheese and a sauce.

01:10:48.790 --> 01:10:52.873
It's so simple that you
don't need anything else.

01:10:55.803 --> 01:10:56.636
How is it?

01:10:57.723 --> 01:11:01.306
- I can see why you'd
use a fork and knife.

01:11:05.738 --> 01:11:07.272
(laughing)

01:11:07.272 --> 01:11:08.328
- So how do you feel?

01:11:08.328 --> 01:11:10.001
- Food coma.
- Food coma?

01:11:10.001 --> 01:11:11.002
- So now's the time.

01:11:11.002 --> 01:11:12.094
You know what time it is.

01:11:12.094 --> 01:11:13.600
- Double cheeseburger dance time.

01:11:13.600 --> 01:11:14.433
Go!

01:11:15.607 --> 01:11:16.947
(laughing)

01:11:16.947 --> 01:11:19.030
Double cheeseburger, hey!

01:11:20.409 --> 01:11:21.242
Oh!

01:11:22.530 --> 01:11:23.888
- Before we get to our Worth It winners,

01:11:23.888 --> 01:11:25.457
what was your favorite thing today

01:11:25.457 --> 01:11:27.054
that wasn't a double cheeseburger?

01:11:27.054 --> 01:11:28.856
- The dessert at Tesse.

01:11:28.856 --> 01:11:30.532
- Oh yeah.
- That was good.

01:11:30.532 --> 01:11:32.347
- You just stole my answer.

01:11:32.347 --> 01:11:34.843
- My favorite thing today that
wasn't a double cheeseburger

01:11:34.843 --> 01:11:36.268
was our co-star Jackson.

01:11:36.268 --> 01:11:37.327
- Aw.
- Aw.

01:11:37.327 --> 01:11:38.908
- And that fact that I
didn't have to eat alone

01:11:38.908 --> 01:11:40.889
with Steven one more time.

01:11:40.889 --> 01:11:42.181
I think we'll start
with you today, Steven.

01:11:42.181 --> 01:11:45.394
Which double cheeseburger
was most worth it to you,

01:11:45.394 --> 01:11:47.382
at its given price?

01:11:47.382 --> 01:11:48.709
- Burgers Never Say Die.

01:11:48.709 --> 01:11:51.953
The texture of the patty
was what I try to accomplish

01:11:51.953 --> 01:11:53.709
when I add chips to my burgers.

01:11:53.709 --> 01:11:55.798
Crunchy, crispy, juicy.

01:11:55.798 --> 01:11:57.218
- Hold on, rewind this back.

01:11:57.218 --> 01:11:59.859
You put potato chips
on your cheeseburgers?

01:11:59.859 --> 01:12:01.588
- Yeah, great double cheeseburger.

01:12:01.588 --> 01:12:03.967
But I gotta say my worth-it winner

01:12:03.967 --> 01:12:07.230
just barely goes to the Burgerlords.

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:09.804
- [Andrew] Oh the vegetarian
double cheeseburger.

01:12:09.804 --> 01:12:13.322
- That redefined what a
double cheeseburger could be.

01:12:13.322 --> 01:12:15.496
Next up, we got Andrew.

01:12:15.496 --> 01:12:17.746
(Andrew groans)

01:12:17.746 --> 01:12:19.541
- I love all of these places.

01:12:19.541 --> 01:12:22.510
Burgers Never Say Die, probably
the best straightforward

01:12:22.510 --> 01:12:23.906
cheeseburger I've ever had.

01:12:23.906 --> 01:12:26.185
Burgerlords, I think that's
where I'd go if I wanted

01:12:26.185 --> 01:12:27.704
to hang out with people.

01:12:27.704 --> 01:12:31.380
Am I gonna say my worth
it winner is Petit Trois?

01:12:31.380 --> 01:12:32.417
I think I am.

01:12:32.417 --> 01:12:34.827
When I think about it, I start salivating.

01:12:34.827 --> 01:12:36.658
I think it's the Bordelaise sauce.

01:12:36.658 --> 01:12:37.788
- Did you see that coming?

01:12:37.788 --> 01:12:38.986
- No.

01:12:38.986 --> 01:12:40.559
- I thought he was gonna
choose Burgers Never Say Die.

01:12:40.559 --> 01:12:41.392
- Yeah, me too
- Look.

01:12:41.392 --> 01:12:43.492
Obviously, they were all great burgers.

01:12:43.492 --> 01:12:45.452
- Okay, before I say my worth it winner,

01:12:45.452 --> 01:12:46.743
how could you not pick the last one?

01:12:46.743 --> 01:12:48.420
I mean you're the hype beast of food.

01:12:48.420 --> 01:12:49.975
- (laughing) What?

01:12:49.975 --> 01:12:51.777
- You're a very fancy boy, you know?

01:12:51.777 --> 01:12:53.485
- Hot dog, you picked the high-priced one.

01:12:53.485 --> 01:12:55.303
Steak, high priced.

01:12:55.303 --> 01:12:56.253
- Wow.

01:12:56.253 --> 01:12:58.186
If you actually go back
and tally the ones I pick

01:12:58.186 --> 01:13:00.777
the high-priced one,
though, it's not that often.

01:13:00.777 --> 01:13:01.861
- Sushi and burger.

01:13:01.861 --> 01:13:03.107
- Okay, all right, I'm out of here.

01:13:03.107 --> 01:13:06.170
- Jackson knowledge of the show
is coming back to haunt us.

01:13:06.170 --> 01:13:07.310
- Just kidding, I love you, buddy.

01:13:07.310 --> 01:13:08.616
- I love you too, bro.

01:13:08.616 --> 01:13:09.886
- All right, Jackson.
- Okay.

01:13:09.886 --> 01:13:11.330
- No more beating around the bush.

01:13:11.330 --> 01:13:12.444
Who's your Worth It winner?

01:13:12.444 --> 01:13:13.695
- [Jackson] So the veggie burger,

01:13:13.695 --> 01:13:15.313
it was a new experience for me.

01:13:15.313 --> 01:13:16.895
I'm going to start
ordering veggie burgers,

01:13:16.895 --> 01:13:18.586
so Burgerlords is my Worth It winner.

01:13:18.586 --> 01:13:19.419
- What?

01:13:19.419 --> 01:13:21.007
(Jackson and Steven exclaim in excitement)

01:13:21.007 --> 01:13:22.932
Are you kidding me?

01:13:22.932 --> 01:13:23.824
That was a good fake out.

01:13:23.824 --> 01:13:25.403
Adam, who's your Worth It winner?

01:13:25.403 --> 01:13:26.644
- [Adam] Burgers Never Say Die.

01:13:26.644 --> 01:13:28.309
- Annie, who's your Worth It winner?

01:13:28.309 --> 01:13:29.142
- Burgers Never Say Die.

01:13:29.142 --> 01:13:30.729
- All right, we've got a good mix here.

01:13:30.729 --> 01:13:33.422
But all we know for sure
is that Andrew lost today.

01:13:33.422 --> 01:13:34.332
- I did not lose.

01:13:34.332 --> 01:13:35.571
- This has been quite the journey.

01:13:35.571 --> 01:13:36.758
- Thank you for having me.

01:13:36.758 --> 01:13:39.139
- No, thank you for flying
all the way out here.

01:13:39.139 --> 01:13:40.411
- It was worth it.

01:13:40.411 --> 01:13:41.582
- What?

01:13:41.582 --> 01:13:43.048
Show name, watch out.

01:13:43.048 --> 01:13:43.881
(laughing)

01:13:43.881 --> 01:13:45.833
This Worth It winner decision
is going to haunt me forever.

01:13:45.833 --> 01:13:47.023
- So you wanna change it?

01:13:47.023 --> 01:13:48.465
- I take it back, it's--

01:13:48.465 --> 01:13:51.048
(upbeat music)

01:13:59.713 --> 01:14:01.691
Back for another episode of Worth It.

01:14:01.691 --> 01:14:03.519
- And we're not just noodling around here,

01:14:03.519 --> 01:14:05.749
but we are covering noodles.

01:14:05.749 --> 01:14:06.582
- Noodles.

01:14:06.582 --> 01:14:09.014
We've done many types
of noodles in the past.

01:14:09.014 --> 01:14:11.447
Pasta, spaghetti, ramen.

01:14:11.447 --> 01:14:16.447
Today, we're focusing on
non-Italian noodles in Los Angeles.

01:14:17.223 --> 01:14:18.056
- Today on Worth It,

01:14:18.056 --> 01:14:20.497
we're going to be trying
three delicious noodle dishes

01:14:20.497 --> 01:14:22.139
at three drastically
different price points

01:14:22.139 --> 01:14:25.495
to find out which noodle is
the most worth it at its price.

01:14:25.495 --> 01:14:28.900
Okay, so we're sticking
specifically to Asian dishes.

01:14:28.900 --> 01:14:30.667
The first place we're going
to, where are we going?

01:14:30.667 --> 01:14:32.840
- We're going to Lao Xi'er Noodle House

01:14:32.840 --> 01:14:34.513
to see Joe and Ellen,

01:14:34.513 --> 01:14:37.235
and we're having their
Wife's Special Noodle dish.

01:14:37.235 --> 01:14:38.068
- Sweet.

01:14:38.068 --> 01:14:38.901
Very, very sweet.

01:14:38.901 --> 01:14:40.526
Like, the story is sweet.

01:14:40.526 --> 01:14:42.162
- It is actually a sweet story.

01:14:42.162 --> 01:14:45.162
(upbeat jazz music)

01:14:46.118 --> 01:14:48.639
How did you come to start
this business together?

01:14:48.639 --> 01:14:49.472
- It's his dream.

01:14:49.472 --> 01:14:52.601
- I can't find my favorite noodle in LA.

01:14:52.601 --> 01:14:54.299
- It was different than
what you were used to?

01:14:54.299 --> 01:14:55.663
- Very different.

01:14:55.663 --> 01:14:58.975
He wanted to keep the original
style from my hometown.

01:14:58.975 --> 01:15:01.652
Shanxi Province, northwest of China.

01:15:01.652 --> 01:15:03.910
- [Andrew] I'm curious
how many styles of noodle

01:15:03.910 --> 01:15:05.393
there are from your hometown?

01:15:05.393 --> 01:15:07.169
- [Ellen] More than a thousand different.

01:15:07.169 --> 01:15:08.809
- More than a thousand different styles?

01:15:08.809 --> 01:15:10.103
- Yeah.
- That's a lot.

01:15:10.103 --> 01:15:13.091
- [Ellen] The cat's ear noodle, Mau Er Duo

01:15:13.091 --> 01:15:15.183
also in my hometown we make a lot.

01:15:15.183 --> 01:15:18.693
Most of the time we make
it with lamb or beef soup.

01:15:18.693 --> 01:15:21.637
Actually, it's very similar
to, like, macaroni noodle.

01:15:21.637 --> 01:15:25.026
Knife-cut noodle is a
flat and thick noodle.

01:15:25.026 --> 01:15:28.014
That noodle we usually make stir-fry.

01:15:28.014 --> 01:15:31.055
- We are focusing on the
Wife's Special Noodle dish.

01:15:31.055 --> 01:15:33.851
Could you tell us what is
the story behind that dish?

01:15:33.851 --> 01:15:35.323
- Introduce this.

01:15:35.323 --> 01:15:36.317
- She's my wife.

01:15:36.317 --> 01:15:37.528
(both laugh loudly)

01:15:37.528 --> 01:15:41.611
(Joe speaks in foreign language)

01:15:57.925 --> 01:16:00.459
- [Ellen] The noodle
is called Helao-style.

01:16:00.459 --> 01:16:02.622
It's a long and thin noodle.

01:16:02.622 --> 01:16:05.905
The machine push it to the pot directly,

01:16:05.905 --> 01:16:07.578
so it's very very fresh.

01:16:07.578 --> 01:16:10.491
It really only take two or
three minutes to cook it.

01:16:10.491 --> 01:16:12.687
The first sauce, the beef

01:16:12.687 --> 01:16:15.424
taste will be a little bit spicy

01:16:15.424 --> 01:16:16.790
and the tomato with egg,

01:16:16.790 --> 01:16:19.861
we just use tomato fried with egg.

01:16:19.861 --> 01:16:22.618
A little bit sour tasting.

01:16:22.618 --> 01:16:27.600
The ground pork just soybean
paste with ground pork.

01:16:27.600 --> 01:16:29.039
- [Andrew] If I'm
understanding this correctly,

01:16:29.039 --> 01:16:31.040
in your hometown it's common to eat

01:16:31.040 --> 01:16:33.455
each of these sauces with noodles.

01:16:33.455 --> 01:16:35.618
Sometimes people will combine two,

01:16:35.618 --> 01:16:37.749
and you had the idea to
put all three together.

01:16:37.749 --> 01:16:38.582
- [Ellen] Yeah.

01:16:38.582 --> 01:16:39.415
- Why was that?

01:16:39.415 --> 01:16:41.658
- [Ellen] The flavor will be more complex

01:16:41.658 --> 01:16:43.232
- [Joe] Because I like this flavor.

01:16:43.232 --> 01:16:44.481
Together have a vegetable,

01:16:44.481 --> 01:16:48.327
and a meat, have a sour, have a salty.

01:16:48.327 --> 01:16:50.632
One bowl is great.

01:16:50.632 --> 01:16:51.465
(cash register dings)

01:16:51.465 --> 01:16:52.926
- You guys also make dumplings here.

01:16:52.926 --> 01:16:54.976
Do you have a recommendation
on which one we should try?

01:16:54.976 --> 01:16:56.586
- Yeah, lamb dumpling, I like.

01:16:56.586 --> 01:16:58.114
New item for me.

01:16:58.114 --> 01:17:00.929
- [Steven] I'm gonna do it
with vinegar and chile oil.

01:17:00.929 --> 01:17:02.963
- Dumpling cheers.
- Cheers.

01:17:02.963 --> 01:17:05.795
(laughs) Go to the bath together.

01:17:05.795 --> 01:17:06.628
Come on.

01:17:09.982 --> 01:17:10.982
- [Both] Mm.

01:17:12.859 --> 01:17:14.738
- I have to have another.

01:17:14.738 --> 01:17:16.405
- Share some with...

01:17:17.658 --> 01:17:18.501
Oh there it is.

01:17:18.501 --> 01:17:20.334
- Mm.
- Want one Annie?

01:17:21.815 --> 01:17:23.022
It's a very soft lamb flavor.

01:17:23.022 --> 01:17:24.183
- [Steven] Very light.

01:17:24.183 --> 01:17:25.016
- Yeah.

01:17:25.016 --> 01:17:26.683
Yidi hasn't had one.

01:17:27.564 --> 01:17:29.483
- Actually, the most important thing

01:17:29.483 --> 01:17:31.247
about this dumpling, the wrapper.

01:17:31.247 --> 01:17:34.334
- Which is the same dough as the noodles.

01:17:34.334 --> 01:17:35.628
- This is a lot of food.

01:17:35.628 --> 01:17:37.768
- This is a fun amount.

01:17:37.768 --> 01:17:39.813
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

01:17:39.813 --> 01:17:42.368
(slurping sounds)

01:17:42.368 --> 01:17:43.887
- Delicious noodle, yeah.

01:17:43.887 --> 01:17:45.288
- I always love a noodle

01:17:45.288 --> 01:17:48.371
that you can just stuff in your face.

01:17:49.258 --> 01:17:50.301
- [Adam] You guys are monsters.

01:17:50.301 --> 01:17:51.581
- Are we monsters?
- I'm sorry.

01:17:51.581 --> 01:17:53.942
- I see Annie going like
this with the headphones.

01:17:53.942 --> 01:17:56.011
It's all right, it's a
lot of slurping going on.

01:17:56.011 --> 01:17:59.125
- I honestly have never heard
of tomato egg before today.

01:17:59.125 --> 01:18:01.083
- This is kind of crazy
because it's like combining

01:18:01.083 --> 01:18:04.295
all of these flavors from my childhood.

01:18:04.295 --> 01:18:06.901
Just all of those things
are just like, whoa.

01:18:06.901 --> 01:18:09.275
You just like (exhales).

01:18:09.275 --> 01:18:10.987
- [Annie] Steven, that's Ratatouille.

01:18:10.987 --> 01:18:12.520
- That's Ratatouille?

01:18:12.520 --> 01:18:14.025
I still haven't seen that movie.

01:18:14.025 --> 01:18:16.625
(slurping sounds)

01:18:16.625 --> 01:18:18.998
- A thousand types of
noodles, approximately.

01:18:18.998 --> 01:18:19.966
- So comforting.

01:18:19.966 --> 01:18:21.312
Very, very good noodle dish.

01:18:21.312 --> 01:18:23.832
- Before we go to our next noodle place,

01:18:23.832 --> 01:18:26.101
we're going to a place called Bistro Na's

01:18:26.101 --> 01:18:28.275
that does Beijing specialty desserts.

01:18:28.275 --> 01:18:29.601
- Yes.

01:18:29.601 --> 01:18:33.298
- And this place is recommended
by our good friend Yidi.

01:18:33.298 --> 01:18:35.237
Do you want to finish that before we,

01:18:35.237 --> 01:18:37.954
yeah this is the biggest
Lazy Susan I've ever seen.

01:18:37.954 --> 01:18:39.707
- Choo-choo.

01:18:39.707 --> 01:18:40.636
- Okay.

01:18:40.636 --> 01:18:42.088
I said, "Yidi, where should
we get some deserts?"

01:18:42.088 --> 01:18:44.365
And she said, "You have
to go to Bistro Na's."

01:18:44.365 --> 01:18:45.233
Why is that?

01:18:45.233 --> 01:18:47.491
- They have food from my hometown Beijing.

01:18:47.491 --> 01:18:50.174
I eat here a lot to cure my homesick,

01:18:50.174 --> 01:18:53.399
and their dessert is
something I grew up eating

01:18:53.399 --> 01:18:55.528
so I figured you guys should try it.

01:18:55.528 --> 01:18:56.361
- [Andrew] Wow.

01:18:56.361 --> 01:18:57.220
- [Steven] Okay.

01:18:57.220 --> 01:18:58.266
- Here you go, guys.

01:18:58.266 --> 01:18:59.809
- So this is how Yidi lives.

01:18:59.809 --> 01:19:01.702
I know exactly what I want,

01:19:01.702 --> 01:19:04.077
but I want to ask Yidi what we should eat.

01:19:04.077 --> 01:19:05.223
- The donkey roll.

01:19:05.223 --> 01:19:06.056
- [Andrew] The donkey roll.

01:19:06.056 --> 01:19:06.889
- [Yidi] Yes.

01:19:06.889 --> 01:19:09.168
- [Andrew] Okay, do you want
to start with the donkey roll?

01:19:09.168 --> 01:19:11.691
- That's not the best
one Yidi, you're wrong.

01:19:11.691 --> 01:19:13.668
I'm a sesame roll kind of guy.

01:19:13.668 --> 01:19:16.577
(soft jazzy music)

01:19:16.577 --> 01:19:17.410
- Mm.

01:19:18.368 --> 01:19:19.285
- Mm, good.

01:19:20.466 --> 01:19:23.105
- Before we enjoy the
rest of these deserts,

01:19:23.105 --> 01:19:24.575
let's enjoy a noodle fact.

01:19:24.575 --> 01:19:26.883
- Noodle fact for your noodle.

01:19:26.883 --> 01:19:29.761
- Oh wow, aren't all facts noodle facts?

01:19:29.761 --> 01:19:30.594
- Yeah.

01:19:30.594 --> 01:19:32.595
- Because they improve your noodle.

01:19:32.595 --> 01:19:34.851
- I did that joke just now.

01:19:34.851 --> 01:19:36.593
- The Chinese, generally speaking,

01:19:36.593 --> 01:19:39.182
don't dry their pasta, but Italians do.

01:19:39.182 --> 01:19:41.707
- I've never made that distinction.

01:19:41.707 --> 01:19:45.244
- For our spaghetti episode,
we saw spaghetti extruded

01:19:45.244 --> 01:19:47.880
but they allowed it to dry before boiling.

01:19:47.880 --> 01:19:51.336
Whereas at Lao Xi'er, it
extruded straight into the water.

01:19:51.336 --> 01:19:53.346
I think it's because it's not optimized

01:19:53.346 --> 01:19:56.494
for that al dente flavor
that you get from drying.

01:19:56.494 --> 01:20:00.068
- It explains so much of why I like

01:20:00.068 --> 01:20:02.072
Chinese noodles more than pasta.

01:20:02.072 --> 01:20:02.905
- Yeah?

01:20:02.905 --> 01:20:03.788
- 'Cause they're just--

01:20:03.788 --> 01:20:04.621
- Squishier.

01:20:04.621 --> 01:20:06.062
- Squishier, yes.

01:20:06.062 --> 01:20:07.614
- So for our next noodle stop,

01:20:07.614 --> 01:20:10.597
we're going to see Bugra
at Dolan's Uyghur Cuisine

01:20:10.597 --> 01:20:12.868
where we're going to be trying
their Big Plate Chicken.

01:20:12.868 --> 01:20:14.004
Before we have that,

01:20:14.004 --> 01:20:15.707
we're gonna eat the
rest of these desserts.

01:20:15.707 --> 01:20:19.374
- Okay so cut the camera,
let us go to town.

01:20:20.369 --> 01:20:21.338
- I like it.

01:20:21.338 --> 01:20:22.171
- Mm.

01:20:23.327 --> 01:20:27.563
(upbeat instrumental music)

01:20:27.563 --> 01:20:28.903
What is Uyghur cuisine?

01:20:28.903 --> 01:20:30.687
- The Uyghur region is located as

01:20:30.687 --> 01:20:32.368
middle point of the Silk Road.

01:20:32.368 --> 01:20:33.939
It's influenced by the Persian,

01:20:33.939 --> 01:20:36.381
the Indian, Turkish and Chinese.

01:20:36.381 --> 01:20:38.295
Many people ask, "You guys have kebab,

01:20:38.295 --> 01:20:40.198
and you guys have some wok dish?"

01:20:40.198 --> 01:20:41.551
"How did you invent this?"

01:20:41.551 --> 01:20:43.703
But actually this is original Uyghur food.

01:20:43.703 --> 01:20:46.345
Our chef, Alerken, is going
to do all dishes today.

01:20:46.345 --> 01:20:48.426
- What are the highlight
dishes of Uyghur cuisine?

01:20:48.426 --> 01:20:51.404
- In ancient times, the Uyghur
people do kebab in desert.

01:20:51.404 --> 01:20:53.770
So you make a fire, and
you make that tower,

01:20:53.770 --> 01:20:56.094
and its shape is like that fire.

01:20:56.094 --> 01:20:59.933
Polo is very traditional
braised rice and meats,

01:20:59.933 --> 01:21:01.960
carrots, raisins together.

01:21:01.960 --> 01:21:05.105
Manta, it looks like
Chinese dumping but bigger.

01:21:05.105 --> 01:21:06.626
The dough is very thin.

01:21:06.626 --> 01:21:09.289
It's stuffed with beef
and onions and spices

01:21:09.289 --> 01:21:12.127
- So talking about the Big Plate Chicken.

01:21:12.127 --> 01:21:13.150
So I imagine it's big.

01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:14.044
- Yes, it's huge.

01:21:14.044 --> 01:21:15.668
- I thought this was a
noodle episode, Andrew.

01:21:15.668 --> 01:21:16.966
- This is a noodle dish, right?

01:21:16.966 --> 01:21:18.405
- Of course, yeah, it comes with noodle.

01:21:18.405 --> 01:21:22.990
This is like very typical
Chinese and Uyghur fusion.

01:21:22.990 --> 01:21:26.558
We fry the chicken with the
oil, and peppers and spices:

01:21:26.558 --> 01:21:30.249
cinnamon, cumin, star
anise, sichuan pepper.

01:21:30.249 --> 01:21:31.876
So this is spicy.

01:21:31.876 --> 01:21:34.721
We put the potato and
we braise half an hour.

01:21:34.721 --> 01:21:36.815
We also make the handmade noodle.

01:21:36.815 --> 01:21:38.921
Noodle, it should be chewy.

01:21:38.921 --> 01:21:42.790
We make the noodle with
water, salt and egg whites.

01:21:42.790 --> 01:21:44.356
- Egg whites in the dough?
- Egg whites, yes.

01:21:44.356 --> 01:21:46.989
Because it makes it really
chewy, and it's thick noodle.

01:21:46.989 --> 01:21:49.475
This is the difference between
Big Plate Chicken noodle

01:21:49.475 --> 01:21:50.792
with the other noodle dish.

01:21:50.792 --> 01:21:52.480
It should be thick and wide.

01:21:52.480 --> 01:21:54.184
- [Steven] I noticed with
the Big Plate Chicken,

01:21:54.184 --> 01:21:57.080
all the noodles are covered
by the things on top.

01:21:57.080 --> 01:21:59.668
Why are you trying to
build this mountain of meat

01:21:59.668 --> 01:22:00.813
on top of the noodles?

01:22:00.813 --> 01:22:03.372
- Because the Big Plate
Chicken, it has soup,

01:22:03.372 --> 01:22:05.682
so when you eat the noodle,
mix it with the soup.

01:22:05.682 --> 01:22:07.709
It's like, amazing taste.

01:22:07.709 --> 01:22:10.483
(cash register dings)

01:22:10.483 --> 01:22:12.411
- [Andrew] I have never seen
a teacup like this before.

01:22:12.411 --> 01:22:13.319
- [Bugra] All are handmade.

01:22:13.319 --> 01:22:14.152
- Wow.

01:22:14.152 --> 01:22:15.133
- Handmade?
- Yeah.

01:22:15.133 --> 01:22:17.716
Flower tea, this is very
common in my country.

01:22:17.716 --> 01:22:19.702
This is also sort of my creation.

01:22:19.702 --> 01:22:20.535
- Cheers.

01:22:20.535 --> 01:22:23.202
- Cheers.
- Don't spill the tea.

01:22:24.058 --> 01:22:25.435
- Mm, oh yeah.

01:22:25.435 --> 01:22:26.299
- Ooh.

01:22:26.299 --> 01:22:27.337
- Rose.

01:22:27.337 --> 01:22:28.170
- Is there cinnamon in here?

01:22:28.170 --> 01:22:29.086
- Yes.
- Cinnamon.

01:22:29.086 --> 01:22:29.919
- Cardamom.

01:22:29.919 --> 01:22:30.752
- [Bugra] Cardamom.

01:22:30.752 --> 01:22:32.460
- You ready for some Big
Plate Chicken Steven?

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:34.278
- Big Plate Chicken, here we go.

01:22:34.278 --> 01:22:35.111
- My goodness.

01:22:35.111 --> 01:22:37.725
Okay we got one noodle
sticking out over here.

01:22:37.725 --> 01:22:39.136
Should we start with chicken?
- Yeah.

01:22:39.136 --> 01:22:40.602
- Since this is Big Plate Chicken?

01:22:40.602 --> 01:22:41.645
Wow that smells good.

01:22:41.645 --> 01:22:43.448
(clicks tongue)

01:22:43.448 --> 01:22:45.878
Mm, that's a little spicy, yeah.

01:22:45.878 --> 01:22:48.623
- The taste that comes with it, linger.

01:22:48.623 --> 01:22:49.798
- [Andrew] There we go, a noodle.

01:22:49.798 --> 01:22:50.955
- Oh, I want one of
those kinds of noodles.

01:22:50.955 --> 01:22:53.748
That one's been sitting below
the weight of the chicken

01:22:53.748 --> 01:22:54.598
Got it, I got it here.

01:22:54.598 --> 01:22:55.941
- It's delicious looking, right?

01:22:55.941 --> 01:22:58.258
(laughs)

01:22:58.258 --> 01:22:59.675
- Cheers
- Cheers

01:23:01.021 --> 01:23:02.341
Oh, man.

01:23:02.341 --> 01:23:03.174
- Whoa.

01:23:03.174 --> 01:23:05.431
- The noodle itself is very dumpling like,

01:23:05.431 --> 01:23:06.793
- Dumpling like.
- in it's softness.

01:23:06.793 --> 01:23:09.562
- It's like you're eating
a dumpling in one noodle.

01:23:09.562 --> 01:23:11.456
- [Andrew] It really is
like a soup at the bottom.

01:23:11.456 --> 01:23:13.038
- I know, why don't we have a spoon?

01:23:13.038 --> 01:23:15.696
- I think it's because
the noodle is your spoon.

01:23:15.696 --> 01:23:17.133
(spoons clank)

01:23:17.133 --> 01:23:18.853
- (laughs) Sound of spoons.

01:23:18.853 --> 01:23:20.176
- As we say that.

01:23:20.176 --> 01:23:21.479
Thank you so much.

01:23:21.479 --> 01:23:22.312
See this peppercorn here?

01:23:22.312 --> 01:23:24.360
It's all going in.

01:23:24.360 --> 01:23:25.619
Oh that's so good.
- Really?

01:23:25.619 --> 01:23:28.468
Okay I need to try it now.
- You gotta do it.

01:23:28.468 --> 01:23:29.685
Focus in on the tongue.

01:23:29.685 --> 01:23:30.831
- The heat builds a little bit.

01:23:30.831 --> 01:23:32.599
The numbing peppercorn
is there a little bit.

01:23:32.599 --> 01:23:33.432
- I love that, yeah.

01:23:33.432 --> 01:23:34.631
- The addition of cinnamon

01:23:34.631 --> 01:23:36.747
is really surprising and delightful.

01:23:36.747 --> 01:23:39.042
- But it reminds me of Malaysian cuisine.

01:23:39.042 --> 01:23:42.608
A lot of the dishes there
are literally marriages.

01:23:42.608 --> 01:23:44.049
There's no better description of this

01:23:44.049 --> 01:23:46.018
than a marriage of dishes.

01:23:46.018 --> 01:23:48.254
- [Andrew] It's delicious.

01:23:48.254 --> 01:23:49.087
- I get it.

01:23:49.087 --> 01:23:51.485
I get why they say stop noodling around,

01:23:51.485 --> 01:23:54.358
because it takes a long
time to eat a noodle.

01:23:54.358 --> 01:23:55.281
Is that it?

01:23:55.281 --> 01:23:57.220
Sometimes when you get out of a movie

01:23:57.220 --> 01:23:59.196
and you're like, "I
don't know what to say."

01:23:59.196 --> 01:24:01.218
- Like when you finish a good
book and you just need to

01:24:01.218 --> 01:24:03.299
take a walk and do nothing.

01:24:03.299 --> 01:24:05.573
- Or like a one and a half
hour drive across Los Angeles

01:24:05.573 --> 01:24:07.200
all the way to Beverly Hills.

01:24:07.200 --> 01:24:08.606
- Cause that's where we're going next,

01:24:08.606 --> 01:24:09.778
but before that happens--

01:24:09.778 --> 01:24:10.879
- Noodle fact.

01:24:10.879 --> 01:24:12.717
Historians believe that
Chinese noodles originated

01:24:12.717 --> 01:24:14.918
more than X years ago.

01:24:14.918 --> 01:24:15.751
What is X?

01:24:16.587 --> 01:24:17.919
- 1500 years ago.

01:24:17.919 --> 01:24:20.310
- Now double that and add 1000.

01:24:20.310 --> 01:24:22.992
4000 years ago.
- 4000 years ago?

01:24:22.992 --> 01:24:25.825
I wonder what the first shape was.

01:24:26.759 --> 01:24:27.746
You find that funny, Annie?

01:24:27.746 --> 01:24:28.856
- I never thought of that before.

01:24:28.856 --> 01:24:31.149
- Yeah, what was the first noodle shape?

01:24:31.149 --> 01:24:32.617
- Oh, noodle shape?
- Oh noodle shape!

01:24:32.617 --> 01:24:33.982
- I thought you were just
talking about shapes.

01:24:33.982 --> 01:24:35.307
- Yeah, me too.

01:24:35.307 --> 01:24:36.523
- [Adam] China has been making bread

01:24:36.523 --> 01:24:38.688
longer than they've been making noodles,

01:24:38.688 --> 01:24:41.526
and noodle making stemmed
from the ripping of dough

01:24:41.526 --> 01:24:42.771
into boiling water.

01:24:42.771 --> 01:24:43.938
- [Both] Whoa.

01:24:45.881 --> 01:24:48.238
- Okay, going across town.

01:24:48.238 --> 01:24:49.764
♪ Making my way across town ♪

01:24:49.764 --> 01:24:51.462
Now we're headed to Crustacean,

01:24:51.462 --> 01:24:54.421
a landmark Vietnamese-American spot.

01:24:54.421 --> 01:24:57.131
We're going to be having
An's Famous Garlic Noodles,

01:24:57.131 --> 01:24:59.612
and on top of that, Dungeness crab.

01:24:59.612 --> 01:25:00.900
The noodles are the creation

01:25:00.900 --> 01:25:03.200
of the founder of the
restaurant, Helene An,

01:25:03.200 --> 01:25:05.040
and she's kept the recipe a secret.

01:25:05.040 --> 01:25:06.759
So they are made in a secret kitchen

01:25:06.759 --> 01:25:07.869
and we can't go in there.

01:25:07.869 --> 01:25:09.279
- We'll be speaking with Chef Tony,

01:25:09.279 --> 01:25:11.440
maybe he can spill the
beans on these noodles.

01:25:11.440 --> 01:25:13.584
- That's okay, I mean,
Oprah actually filmed here

01:25:13.584 --> 01:25:15.133
and she wasn't allowed in there,

01:25:15.133 --> 01:25:17.258
so we're pretty much Oprah.

01:25:17.258 --> 01:25:18.814
(everybody laughs)

01:25:18.814 --> 01:25:22.744
(upbeat electronic music)

01:25:22.744 --> 01:25:24.856
- I understand that your kitchen operates

01:25:24.856 --> 01:25:25.919
somewhat in secret.

01:25:25.919 --> 01:25:26.752
- Somewhat.

01:25:26.752 --> 01:25:28.967
We have a kitchen within a kitchen.

01:25:28.967 --> 01:25:30.800
That kitchen's for family members

01:25:30.800 --> 01:25:32.592
and people that have
worked in the restaurant

01:25:32.592 --> 01:25:33.689
for over 10 years.

01:25:33.689 --> 01:25:34.659
- Why?

01:25:34.659 --> 01:25:37.306
- My boss, Chef Helene
An, she first started

01:25:37.306 --> 01:25:39.518
way back in the 70's in this little deli,

01:25:39.518 --> 01:25:42.685
and slowly putting Asian
flairs here and there.

01:25:42.685 --> 01:25:44.176
She knew she was on to something,

01:25:44.176 --> 01:25:46.304
so she wanted to keep
it within the family.

01:25:46.304 --> 01:25:48.571
We have about four items that
come from secret originally.

01:25:48.571 --> 01:25:50.989
The garlic noodles have been
around since we've been open.

01:25:50.989 --> 01:25:53.506
She saw that everybody
loves spaghetti and Parmesan

01:25:53.506 --> 01:25:55.108
so that was her inspiration.

01:25:55.108 --> 01:25:57.337
She was just named the
mother of Asian fusion.

01:25:57.337 --> 01:25:58.988
This is the signature
dish of the restaurant.

01:25:58.988 --> 01:26:01.573
- Asian fusion has been looked
at in a variety of ways.

01:26:01.573 --> 01:26:03.096
Where I grew up, it was looked down on,

01:26:03.096 --> 01:26:04.316
but you're doing Asian fusion,

01:26:04.316 --> 01:26:06.158
and it's right here in Beverly Hills.

01:26:06.158 --> 01:26:08.628
How are you able to flip the
narrative of Asian fusion food?

01:26:08.628 --> 01:26:09.973
- I think it was just something cool

01:26:09.973 --> 01:26:11.727
for people to hate on fusion,

01:26:11.727 --> 01:26:13.900
but the way I became
very comfortable about it

01:26:13.900 --> 01:26:15.741
is when I spoke with Helene.

01:26:15.741 --> 01:26:17.202
She didn't really know what fusion was.

01:26:17.202 --> 01:26:18.247
She was just like, "I'm just cooking

01:26:18.247 --> 01:26:19.316
the way I want to cook."

01:26:19.316 --> 01:26:21.141
Now we're grabbing from
different regions of the world,

01:26:21.141 --> 01:26:23.836
as long as you're doing it intelligently,

01:26:23.836 --> 01:26:25.850
the end result should be pretty tasty.

01:26:25.850 --> 01:26:28.525
- So this is our last stop in our video,

01:26:28.525 --> 01:26:29.870
it's the three dollar signs,

01:26:29.870 --> 01:26:32.717
but the noodles themselves are not really

01:26:32.717 --> 01:26:34.060
the expensive thing here, right?

01:26:34.060 --> 01:26:35.135
- Generally if you're getting a crab

01:26:35.135 --> 01:26:36.671
you're always getting a garlic noodle,

01:26:36.671 --> 01:26:38.639
but they're two separate
items on the menu.

01:26:38.639 --> 01:26:41.876
I would say 99.9% of
people that get the crab,

01:26:41.876 --> 01:26:42.709
they always get a noodle.

01:26:42.709 --> 01:26:44.968
- To enjoy the noodles to their best.

01:26:44.968 --> 01:26:46.833
- This is not a question,
you have to get the crab.

01:26:46.833 --> 01:26:47.683
(both laugh)

01:26:47.683 --> 01:26:48.516
- Gotcha.

01:26:48.516 --> 01:26:52.408
(cash register dings)

01:26:52.408 --> 01:26:53.241
- Whoa.

01:26:54.120 --> 01:26:56.549
This smells like my fire
alarm is about to go off.

01:26:56.549 --> 01:26:57.382
(laughs)

01:26:57.382 --> 01:26:58.809
- Look at that.

01:26:58.809 --> 01:27:00.672
It feels like something you
shouldn't do at a restaurant

01:27:00.672 --> 01:27:04.238
this nice but I do just
want to go (throaty laugh).

01:27:04.238 --> 01:27:05.571
- Caviar cheers.

01:27:07.024 --> 01:27:09.240
- Oh man, that's so awesome.

01:27:09.240 --> 01:27:11.014
- Actually, that makes a lot of sense.

01:27:11.014 --> 01:27:11.847
- Here we go.

01:27:11.847 --> 01:27:12.717
- Here we go.

01:27:12.717 --> 01:27:13.911
Ah, there's the hit of garlic.

01:27:13.911 --> 01:27:15.761
It takes about two seconds.

01:27:15.761 --> 01:27:17.106
- [Tony] One crab in the shell,

01:27:17.106 --> 01:27:18.917
and one crab picked out the shell.

01:27:18.917 --> 01:27:20.302
- Nice.
- Wow.

01:27:20.302 --> 01:27:21.135
And bibs?

01:27:21.135 --> 01:27:21.968
No way.

01:27:21.968 --> 01:27:22.801
- Yes!

01:27:23.851 --> 01:27:27.331
This is way more simple than I thought.

01:27:27.331 --> 01:27:28.164
- [Andrew] Right.

01:27:28.164 --> 01:27:28.997
- Cheers.

01:27:32.279 --> 01:27:33.886
- Oh yeah, that's really good.

01:27:33.886 --> 01:27:36.286
I just want to eat this whole bowl.

01:27:36.286 --> 01:27:37.703
- It's so fluffy.

01:27:38.583 --> 01:27:40.786
Squishy, fluffy noodle.
- Fluffy noodle, yeah.

01:27:40.786 --> 01:27:42.229
I'm gonna do a little bit of crab.

01:27:42.229 --> 01:27:43.266
- [Steven] Isn't this the life?

01:27:43.266 --> 01:27:44.648
Pre-picked crab.

01:27:44.648 --> 01:27:47.815
(sensual blues music)

01:27:55.901 --> 01:27:57.042
- It's really good, right?

01:27:57.042 --> 01:27:59.384
- This is my fantasy as a kid

01:27:59.384 --> 01:28:01.152
of what a dish should taste like.

01:28:01.152 --> 01:28:04.456
- This is so close to the buttered noodles

01:28:04.456 --> 01:28:05.934
you would eat as a kid, right?

01:28:05.934 --> 01:28:08.908
Add on the best version
of a buttery thing,

01:28:08.908 --> 01:28:10.195
buttery crab meat.

01:28:10.195 --> 01:28:12.140
- Pre-picked crab, okay,

01:28:12.140 --> 01:28:15.033
is like birds who get to eat food

01:28:15.033 --> 01:28:16.943
that's already been chewed
up for them by their parents.

01:28:16.943 --> 01:28:19.309
- Talking about when a
mama bird chews up food

01:28:19.309 --> 01:28:22.320
and then throws it up into
the chickling's mouth.

01:28:22.320 --> 01:28:25.494
- The idea of having
food prepared for you.

01:28:25.494 --> 01:28:28.024
- It's a mothers love, right?

01:28:28.024 --> 01:28:28.857
It's great.

01:28:28.857 --> 01:28:31.504
- Now how do you make
it, that's the question.

01:28:31.504 --> 01:28:34.587
(somber blues music)

01:28:36.732 --> 01:28:38.174
Long noodles, long life.

01:28:38.174 --> 01:28:39.087
- Long day.

01:28:39.087 --> 01:28:40.005
- That's what they say.

01:28:40.005 --> 01:28:41.652
One thing that was your favorite thing

01:28:41.652 --> 01:28:43.271
that was not a noodle today?

01:28:43.271 --> 01:28:46.321
- The noodle extruder at
Lao Xi'er Noodle House

01:28:46.321 --> 01:28:50.473
that was like a old
fashioned manual train cart.

01:28:50.473 --> 01:28:54.196
- At Crustacean, below the
floor, there's a fish tank

01:28:54.196 --> 01:28:57.061
and there's a lot of beautiful
Koi fish living down there.

01:28:57.061 --> 01:28:59.564
Okay Andrew, which noodle
was the most worth it

01:28:59.564 --> 01:29:00.397
at it's given price?

01:29:00.397 --> 01:29:02.377
- I'll give a honorable mention to

01:29:02.377 --> 01:29:04.918
the Cat's Ear Noodle from Lao Xi'er.

01:29:04.918 --> 01:29:05.966
My Worth It winner is gonna go to

01:29:05.966 --> 01:29:07.410
Dolan's Uyghur Cuisine,

01:29:07.410 --> 01:29:08.288
- Oh my gosh.
- So good.

01:29:08.288 --> 01:29:10.486
- Oh my gosh, I can't believe
it, we agree for once.

01:29:10.486 --> 01:29:12.786
I love Crustacean, and I
was gonna say Lao Xi'er

01:29:12.786 --> 01:29:15.994
because the vast experiences
you get in one bowl.

01:29:15.994 --> 01:29:18.176
My Worth It winner goes
to Dolan's Uyghur Cuisine.

01:29:18.176 --> 01:29:20.028
- Adam, who is your Worth It winner?

01:29:20.028 --> 01:29:20.861
- [Adam] Lao Xi'er.

01:29:20.861 --> 01:29:22.821
- Annie, who is your
favorite noodle from today?

01:29:22.821 --> 01:29:23.654
- Crustacean

01:29:23.654 --> 01:29:25.131
- [Andrew] And Yidi, while
we're in the neighborhood.

01:29:25.131 --> 01:29:26.455
- Donkey Roll!

01:29:26.455 --> 01:29:27.799
(laughs)

01:29:27.799 --> 01:29:29.289
- Well that does it for episode two

01:29:29.289 --> 01:29:31.076
in our end of year miniseries.

01:29:31.076 --> 01:29:34.233
Come back next week for
a very crispy conclusion.

01:29:34.233 --> 01:29:36.020
- It also has soft ends.

01:29:36.020 --> 01:29:37.030
- [Adam] Fried Chicken Sandwich.

01:29:37.030 --> 01:29:38.233
- Aw, come on Adam.

01:29:38.233 --> 01:29:41.316
(gentle piano music)

01:29:49.993 --> 01:29:51.623
- All right Steven, this will be

01:29:51.623 --> 01:29:54.898
our final Worth It episode for 2019.

01:29:54.898 --> 01:29:55.731
- Today on Worth It,

01:29:55.731 --> 01:29:58.261
we're going to be trying
three fried chicken sandwiches

01:29:58.261 --> 01:30:00.014
at three drastically
different price points

01:30:00.014 --> 01:30:02.639
to find out which one is the
most Worth It at its price.

01:30:02.639 --> 01:30:04.471
- I think we're
experiencing, at the moment,

01:30:04.471 --> 01:30:07.644
a little bit of a fried
chicken sandwich craze.

01:30:07.644 --> 01:30:09.225
We thought we'd investigate the issue

01:30:09.225 --> 01:30:11.213
and do fried chicken sandwiches ourselves.

01:30:11.213 --> 01:30:13.421
- I like what you said, "investigate."

01:30:13.421 --> 01:30:15.233
- Oh look at that, fresh asphalts.

01:30:15.233 --> 01:30:18.491
- The way you say words (laughs)

01:30:18.491 --> 01:30:20.048
- It's look golden and delicious

01:30:20.048 --> 01:30:21.607
the way fresh fried chicken does.

01:30:21.607 --> 01:30:23.048
Doesn't that look delicious?

01:30:23.048 --> 01:30:25.234
That looks tasty,

01:30:25.234 --> 01:30:26.067
what?

01:30:27.210 --> 01:30:29.462
So the first place we're heading to is

01:30:29.462 --> 01:30:31.879
The Window At American Beauty.

01:30:31.879 --> 01:30:34.119
Chef Elisha is going to be showing us

01:30:34.119 --> 01:30:36.148
their fried chicken sandwich.

01:30:36.148 --> 01:30:37.752
American Beauty is a steakhouse.

01:30:37.752 --> 01:30:40.875
And during the day,
they have a to-go window

01:30:40.875 --> 01:30:43.688
that does things like burgers, fries,

01:30:43.688 --> 01:30:45.371
fried chicken sandwiches, obviously.

01:30:45.371 --> 01:30:48.021
- I like that a lot,
it feels very New York.

01:30:48.021 --> 01:30:49.186
I don't know where I'm going with that.

01:30:49.186 --> 01:30:51.409
I just like it, I just like New York.

01:30:51.409 --> 01:30:53.570
(atmospheric music)

01:30:53.570 --> 01:30:54.452
- What is The Window?

01:30:54.452 --> 01:30:56.245
- So The Window is our daytime operation

01:30:56.245 --> 01:30:57.883
where we serve delicious food

01:30:57.883 --> 01:30:59.013
at a very affordable price.

01:30:59.013 --> 01:30:59.846
- [Steven] As I understand it,

01:30:59.846 --> 01:31:01.233
this chicken sandwich is under six bucks?

01:31:01.233 --> 01:31:03.116
- [Chef Elisha] Yeah, it's
five dollars and 50 cents.

01:31:03.116 --> 01:31:04.157
- Wow.
- That is crazy.

01:31:04.157 --> 01:31:05.570
- I don't know if I'd
say we're competition

01:31:05.570 --> 01:31:07.518
with fast-food, but we're
trying to hit around

01:31:07.518 --> 01:31:09.553
the price point of the
regular fast-food chains.

01:31:09.553 --> 01:31:10.752
We're making everything in house

01:31:10.752 --> 01:31:13.434
and the idea is to deliver
hospitality to our guests,

01:31:13.434 --> 01:31:16.121
whether they order the fried
chicken sandwich at the window

01:31:16.121 --> 01:31:18.194
or they're ordering our steaks at night.

01:31:18.194 --> 01:31:20.917
So we butterfly the
chicken, so it spreads out,

01:31:20.917 --> 01:31:23.374
we pound it so it's the
same thickness around.

01:31:23.374 --> 01:31:25.774
- [Andrew] I saw that you
were also scoring the meat

01:31:25.774 --> 01:31:27.049
before you were pounding it.

01:31:27.049 --> 01:31:28.656
- [Chef Elisha] By scoring
it, we're both helping

01:31:28.656 --> 01:31:30.435
the buttermilk and the
seasoning penetrate,

01:31:30.435 --> 01:31:32.529
but also we're getting a
little bit more surface space,

01:31:32.529 --> 01:31:34.477
so there's a little
bit more crunchy parts.

01:31:34.477 --> 01:31:35.348
And then we just fry it.

01:31:35.348 --> 01:31:37.594
We have the awesome Martin's Potato Rolls.

01:31:37.594 --> 01:31:39.788
The jalapeño aioli,
which gives a little bit

01:31:39.788 --> 01:31:41.268
more fat and some heat.

01:31:41.268 --> 01:31:42.875
And then we have this very simple slaw

01:31:42.875 --> 01:31:44.239
that gives a little freshness.

01:31:44.239 --> 01:31:46.469
Just cabbage, some carrots, red onion,

01:31:46.469 --> 01:31:48.413
and a very simple red wine vinaigrette.

01:31:48.413 --> 01:31:49.696
- [Steven] Is that what you would say

01:31:49.696 --> 01:31:51.012
makes a perfect fried chicken sandwich?

01:31:51.012 --> 01:31:53.392
Is it the balance of all
of those things together?

01:31:53.392 --> 01:31:54.360
- [Chef Elisha] For
me, I think it's really

01:31:54.360 --> 01:31:55.320
the combination of it all.

01:31:55.320 --> 01:31:56.844
It's an evolution while you're eating it.

01:31:56.844 --> 01:31:59.254
And as it goes, it's kinda
all compliments itself.

01:31:59.254 --> 01:32:01.047
And I think that really makes for a better

01:32:01.047 --> 01:32:03.073
and more enjoyable eating experience.

01:32:03.073 --> 01:32:05.086
(cash register dings)

01:32:05.086 --> 01:32:07.171
- [Announcer] Order for Worth It.

01:32:07.171 --> 01:32:08.042
- That's our order!

01:32:08.042 --> 01:32:09.619
- Thank you guys!
- Thank you.

01:32:09.619 --> 01:32:11.373
- Oh, that smells amazing.
- Oh!

01:32:11.373 --> 01:32:13.635
- Sorry, I just want that.
- That was a hard hit.

01:32:13.635 --> 01:32:15.399
There's something about food in a box

01:32:15.399 --> 01:32:17.109
like this that I just love every time.

01:32:17.109 --> 01:32:17.942
- Mm-hmm.

01:32:17.942 --> 01:32:20.684
- It's like the American Bento Box.

01:32:20.684 --> 01:32:21.517
- Nice.

01:32:21.517 --> 01:32:22.603
- Do you eat ketchup with your fries?

01:32:22.603 --> 01:32:23.436
- I do.

01:32:23.436 --> 01:32:27.516
- Okay, hold on, I got you.
- All right.

01:32:27.516 --> 01:32:28.866
I took a fry, I mean what?

01:32:28.866 --> 01:32:29.761
- You what?

01:32:29.761 --> 01:32:31.677
You're lucky I don't dump
this ketchup on your head.

01:32:31.677 --> 01:32:33.377
- Do it, I dare you.

01:32:33.377 --> 01:32:34.519
Fries.

01:32:34.519 --> 01:32:36.346
(video game music)

01:32:36.346 --> 01:32:38.624
- Mm, incredible fry.

01:32:38.624 --> 01:32:40.928
It's crunchy like cereal is crunchy.

01:32:40.928 --> 01:32:42.716
- [Steven] Okay, lets go sando' time.

01:32:42.716 --> 01:32:44.950
- Oh, look at that.
- Whoa!

01:32:44.950 --> 01:32:47.004
It feels fluffier than I thought.

01:32:47.004 --> 01:32:48.249
- That's that potato bun.

01:32:48.249 --> 01:32:49.989
Cheers.
- Cheers.

01:32:49.989 --> 01:32:51.986
(upbeat music)

01:32:51.986 --> 01:32:53.275
- Mm!
- Mm.

01:32:53.275 --> 01:32:55.676
Yeah, that was a really good first bite.

01:32:55.676 --> 01:32:56.926
- [Andrew] Wow.

01:32:58.015 --> 01:32:59.326
- Mm!

01:32:59.326 --> 01:33:00.748
That was a really good second bite.

01:33:00.748 --> 01:33:02.906
- I have a feeling every
bite is going to be good.

01:33:02.906 --> 01:33:04.702
- What I'm realizing about this bun,

01:33:04.702 --> 01:33:08.214
it's so soft and fluffy
that it's doing its job

01:33:08.214 --> 01:33:10.508
in like making the fried chicken

01:33:10.508 --> 01:33:11.899
the real star of the show.

01:33:11.899 --> 01:33:13.729
- So this is the, "why are we having

01:33:13.729 --> 01:33:15.818
fried chicken sandwich"
as a question, you know?

01:33:15.818 --> 01:33:17.500
'Cause we've done fried chicken before,

01:33:17.500 --> 01:33:19.750
but the bun just dials
the whole thing back

01:33:19.750 --> 01:33:21.578
into like soft doughnut territory.

01:33:21.578 --> 01:33:22.868
- Mm-hmm.
- Which I kind of like.

01:33:22.868 --> 01:33:24.253
You know what this does
kind of remind me of?

01:33:24.253 --> 01:33:25.193
- Tell me.
- I think you're going

01:33:25.193 --> 01:33:26.287
to be mad that you didn't think of this.

01:33:26.287 --> 01:33:27.120
- No, I think this is going to be

01:33:27.120 --> 01:33:28.183
less exciting than I thought.

01:33:28.183 --> 01:33:31.667
- The peking duck bun
that we had in New York.

01:33:31.667 --> 01:33:35.660
That soft, squishy bun
with a delicious meat

01:33:35.660 --> 01:33:38.264
and condiment and crispy vegetable.

01:33:38.264 --> 01:33:40.504
Plus the cucumber and the sauce

01:33:40.504 --> 01:33:42.190
that adds a little sweetness.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:33:42.190 --> 01:33:44.248
You're right, okay, yeah.

01:33:44.248 --> 01:33:47.165
(video game music)

01:33:48.990 --> 01:33:53.525
- American Beauty, what a
beautiful time in America.

01:33:53.525 --> 01:33:55.741
- That's too easy for
you, I'm disappointed.

01:33:55.741 --> 01:33:58.038
- Okay, so before our next proper

01:33:58.038 --> 01:33:59.762
fried chicken sandwich,
we're actually gonna

01:33:59.762 --> 01:34:01.587
have a bonus piece of fried chicken.

01:34:01.587 --> 01:34:02.420
(Steven sighs)

01:34:02.420 --> 01:34:03.962
Friend of the show, David Chang,

01:34:03.962 --> 01:34:07.146
has a number of fried chicken
sandwich shops called Fuku.

01:34:07.146 --> 01:34:10.607
They have an off menu,
mega spicy, fried chicken

01:34:10.607 --> 01:34:14.395
designed to taste better
as a cold product.

01:34:14.395 --> 01:34:17.425
It's like a food pun but in the language

01:34:17.425 --> 01:34:19.453
of flavor and temperature.

01:34:19.453 --> 01:34:21.021
- It sounds more like an oxymoron.

01:34:21.021 --> 01:34:22.055
- It's like a fun--

01:34:22.055 --> 01:34:25.450
- You'd have to be an
oxy-moron to eat this.

01:34:25.450 --> 01:34:26.283
Aah!

01:34:27.331 --> 01:34:29.108
- Okay you know what's terrifying?

01:34:29.108 --> 01:34:31.455
Black gloves that come with your food.

01:34:31.455 --> 01:34:33.205
Here it is, oh, whew!

01:34:34.490 --> 01:34:36.345
Put your gloves on. (laughs)

01:34:36.345 --> 01:34:38.028
This guy's walking his
dog and is just looking

01:34:38.028 --> 01:34:40.790
at three guys putting on black gloves.

01:34:40.790 --> 01:34:42.310
(dramatic music)

01:34:42.310 --> 01:34:43.432
Oh my god!

01:34:43.432 --> 01:34:45.349
It looks upsetting.

01:34:45.349 --> 01:34:46.350
- Why!

01:34:46.350 --> 01:34:47.349
Oh!

01:34:47.349 --> 01:34:49.192
- It looks like mummified meat.

01:34:49.192 --> 01:34:52.203
- Why is it so dark? (laughs)

01:34:52.203 --> 01:34:55.445
- Adam, I wish you had that
bottle of ranch on standby.

01:34:55.445 --> 01:34:56.899
- Oh! (laughs)

01:34:56.899 --> 01:34:59.769
- I thought we were just dipping!

01:34:59.769 --> 01:35:00.861
(laughs) Adam!

01:35:00.861 --> 01:35:02.157
This is so bizarre.

01:35:02.157 --> 01:35:03.949
- Oh, it's cold! (laughs)

01:35:03.949 --> 01:35:05.561
- Cheers!

01:35:05.561 --> 01:35:08.283
(dramatic music)

01:35:08.283 --> 01:35:09.717
Oh, it's not too bad.

01:35:09.717 --> 01:35:10.550
Oh!

01:35:11.558 --> 01:35:14.517
- Oh, it's like cinnamon-sugary too.

01:35:14.517 --> 01:35:15.795
- Oh!
- Oh!

01:35:15.795 --> 01:35:16.628
- It's bad.

01:35:16.628 --> 01:35:19.104
- There's pickles that you
can have along side (sighs).

01:35:19.104 --> 01:35:21.766
(sighs) No, I don't like this.

01:35:21.766 --> 01:35:22.941
- Give me the fact!

01:35:22.941 --> 01:35:23.774
- Okay.

01:35:23.774 --> 01:35:25.794
Hot-cold fried chicken fact time.

01:35:25.794 --> 01:35:28.877
Mm, that's good, an ice, slush drink.

01:35:29.857 --> 01:35:30.787
- It's making it worse.

01:35:30.787 --> 01:35:32.676
- Can you say hot-cold fact chicken time?

01:35:32.676 --> 01:35:33.951
- Hot-Cold Fact Chicken time!

01:35:33.951 --> 01:35:34.982
(Adam sneezes)

01:35:34.982 --> 01:35:36.352
(laughs) Oh, wow!

01:35:36.352 --> 01:35:38.338
- The chemical in spicy foods that

01:35:38.338 --> 01:35:41.321
makes something spicy is capsaicin.

01:35:41.321 --> 01:35:44.976
Capsaicin is known to increase
energy, reduce appetite,

01:35:44.976 --> 01:35:48.615
increase salivation,
and release endorphins.

01:35:48.615 --> 01:35:49.931
(laughing)

01:35:49.931 --> 01:35:51.928
- Ability to taste reduces
in higher temperatures,

01:35:51.928 --> 01:35:53.331
above 86 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:35:53.331 --> 01:35:55.639
as well as lower temperatures
below 68 degrees Fahrenheit.

01:35:55.639 --> 01:35:57.277
Oh!

01:35:57.277 --> 01:36:00.105
- I think this chicken might
be right in the center.

01:36:00.105 --> 01:36:01.415
You're still going?

01:36:01.415 --> 01:36:02.631
- Oh my goodness!

01:36:02.631 --> 01:36:04.960
- So we're on our way
downtown to Pikunico.

01:36:04.960 --> 01:36:06.402
We're going to see chef Kuniko

01:36:06.402 --> 01:36:08.456
and have her fried chicken sandwich.

01:36:08.456 --> 01:36:10.085
- Okay, is it spicy?

01:36:10.085 --> 01:36:11.377
- No, it's okay.

01:36:11.377 --> 01:36:12.605
- Okay, good.
- Yeah.

01:36:12.605 --> 01:36:15.571
(atmospheric music)

01:36:15.571 --> 01:36:18.252
- Pikunico means picnic plus Kuniko.

01:36:18.252 --> 01:36:22.090
So everything is in a to-go
box and you can eat outside

01:36:22.090 --> 01:36:24.098
of the restaurant, like a picnic.

01:36:24.098 --> 01:36:25.549
- Why did you choose fried chicken

01:36:25.549 --> 01:36:27.557
as the cornerstone of the menu?

01:36:27.557 --> 01:36:28.535
- I grew up with it.

01:36:28.535 --> 01:36:30.591
My grandma never liked cooking,

01:36:30.591 --> 01:36:32.709
so we'd always go buy it. (laughs)

01:36:32.709 --> 01:36:36.795
When I came here, I ate this
southern style fried chicken,

01:36:36.795 --> 01:36:41.777
with thick, thick crust and I
loved it, so I did a hybrid.

01:36:41.777 --> 01:36:45.337
Light, as Japanese, but
also with a crunchiness.

01:36:45.337 --> 01:36:47.199
Normally it's a wheat flour,

01:36:47.199 --> 01:36:49.639
but we do brown rice and potato starch.

01:36:49.639 --> 01:36:52.591
That's maybe the most special
part of our fried chicken.

01:36:52.591 --> 01:36:55.170
Our fryer don't carry any gluten.

01:36:55.170 --> 01:36:58.028
A lot of my friends' kids,
they have serious allergy,

01:36:58.028 --> 01:36:59.941
and they never eaten fried chicken.

01:36:59.941 --> 01:37:02.359
So I said, okay you can come here.

01:37:02.359 --> 01:37:04.842
- [Andrew] Why did you want
to offer a sandwich option

01:37:04.842 --> 01:37:05.862
for your fried chicken?

01:37:05.862 --> 01:37:08.976
- I'm trying to belong here. (laughs)

01:37:08.976 --> 01:37:10.440
- [Andrew] Do you ever
find those in Japan?

01:37:10.440 --> 01:37:13.475
- [Kuniko] I don't believe
it's a popular thing.

01:37:13.475 --> 01:37:15.383
Japanese just pack rice balls,

01:37:15.383 --> 01:37:17.654
and fried chicken, and pickles.

01:37:17.654 --> 01:37:19.282
And that's our picnic.

01:37:19.282 --> 01:37:21.838
But a lot of people said, "Kuni, come on,

01:37:21.838 --> 01:37:23.483
like you're living in the United States,

01:37:23.483 --> 01:37:26.491
everyone loves sandwich,
come up with a great one."

01:37:26.491 --> 01:37:29.550
I wanted to make the sandwich
as special as I could.

01:37:29.550 --> 01:37:32.847
We created a golden bun
which carries turmeric.

01:37:32.847 --> 01:37:34.920
The turmeric is antiinflammatory.

01:37:34.920 --> 01:37:37.371
So you can lessen your feeling

01:37:37.371 --> 01:37:39.749
of I'm eating fried
chicken today. (laughs)

01:37:39.749 --> 01:37:42.368
To me, sandwich is about combination.

01:37:42.368 --> 01:37:46.921
The soft bun, crunchy chicken,
acid from lemon vinaigrette.

01:37:46.921 --> 01:37:49.405
I want it to have everything in one bite.

01:37:49.405 --> 01:37:51.766
- I mean you say one bite,
but I've seen the sandwich,

01:37:51.766 --> 01:37:54.547
and it's quite tall.
- Yes.

01:37:54.547 --> 01:37:56.304
- How do I eat that with one bite?

01:37:56.304 --> 01:37:58.708
- Smoosh. (laughs)

01:37:58.708 --> 01:38:01.376
(cash register dings)

01:38:01.376 --> 01:38:03.618
- We've come for the sandwiches,
but we also got a salad

01:38:03.618 --> 01:38:04.922
that we're going to try first.

01:38:04.922 --> 01:38:07.368
- [Kuniko] I like my
seaweed and cucumber salad.

01:38:07.368 --> 01:38:10.541
Our seaweed is from Japan
and it's salt cured.

01:38:10.541 --> 01:38:12.998
The texture is not like dehydrated,

01:38:12.998 --> 01:38:15.177
like wakame, like paper thin.

01:38:15.177 --> 01:38:17.310
It's very meaty and satisfying.

01:38:17.310 --> 01:38:19.445
I eat my salad everyday.

01:38:19.445 --> 01:38:20.859
- Cheers.

01:38:20.859 --> 01:38:22.848
(atmospheric music)

01:38:22.848 --> 01:38:24.198
- Mm!
- Wow.

01:38:24.198 --> 01:38:28.167
- I'm not sure I've ever had
this type of seaweed before.

01:38:28.167 --> 01:38:30.806
It is denser and meatier.

01:38:30.806 --> 01:38:33.002
- Pause button, back to the sandwich.

01:38:33.002 --> 01:38:36.572
She gave us good advice to smoosh.

01:38:36.572 --> 01:38:39.339
